  ],
  "contracts": {
    "pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "post-td-first-law-energy",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For every admitted $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ with equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$, fixed material constitution, zero change in center-of-mass kinetic energy, and zero change in separately tracked external-field potential energy in the fixed chart selected by the process model, the adopted model balance is\n$$U_c(x_P^+)-U_c(x_P^-)=Q[P]+W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P],$$\nwhere $U_c$ is the dimensioned energy-coordinate map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]].\n\nHere the internal-energy coordinate is evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame as in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], while $Q[P]$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]$ are the protocol transfers defined by [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], in joules as in [[def-td-si-quantities]]. The sign convention is positive $Q$ into the system and positive $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ for work done on it. If $P$ is adiabatic, its defining condition is $Q[P]=0$, so the same adopted balance gives $W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]=U_c(x_P^+)-U_c(x_P^-)$.\n\nThis equation is an adopted physical postulate of the stated framework. It is not deduced here from a mechanical model or from experimental observations. Records with matter exchange or nonzero changes in omitted kinetic or external-field energy require a separately specified balance and are outside this item.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound, and restrict to constituent splits for which [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] supplies the additive total entropy. The adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^+)-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^-)\\ge0,$$\n\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is the sum of the constituent entropies in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. The process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt zero total entropy change. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n\u201cGlobal\u201d refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [A2] and [F1], $\\Delta U=Q-p_0\\Delta V$. By [A1], $\\Delta S_{\\rm tot}=\\Delta S-Q/T_0$, where $\\Delta S$ is the system entropy change. Therefore\n$$\n\\Delta\\Phi_{T_0,p_0} =\\Delta U+p_0\\Delta V-T_0\\Delta S =Q-T_0\\Delta S =-T_0\\Delta S_{\\rm tot}\\le0\n$$\nby $T_0>0$ and [F2]. The derivation does not depend on whether $\\Delta n_j$ arises from an internal reaction.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the exact fixed-total-energy feasible set, [A1]\u2013[A2] give $E_R=E_{\\rm tot}-U-p_0V$ up to a fixed reference constant. Hence\n$$\nS_{\\rm tot}=S+S_R =\\mathrm{constant}+S-\\frac{U+p_0V}{T_0} =\\mathrm{constant}-\\frac{\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}}{T_0}.\n$$\nSince $T_0>0$, an attained global maximizer of $S_{\\rm tot}$ is exactly a global minimizer of $\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}$ on the induced feasible reaction states, by [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Fix a system at a positive temperature $T_0$ and external pressure $p_0>0$. Boundary empty: The empty boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix a system at a positive temperature $T_0$ and external pressure $p_0>0$. Internal reaction extents may vary in a declared feasible set, but no species crosses the system's outer boundary. The system contacts only an ideal thermal reservoir at $T_0$ and an ideal pressure store at $p_0$; all other work coordinates and excluded-energy changes are absent. Let $U$ be system internal energy in joules, $V$ volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, and $S$ system entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. Define the constrained potential\n$$\n\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}:=U+p_0V-T_0S.\n$$\nFor every admitted spontaneous endpoint change, $\\Delta\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}\\le0$. If the system-plus-reservoir-plus-store endpoint is an attained global entropy maximum on its exact feasible set, then the corresponding system endpoint attains a global minimum of $\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}$ over the induced feasible reaction states. This is a conditional model deduction; it does not assert that arbitrary fixed-$T,p$ reactions have this preparation or that the system reaches the minimum dynamically.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "4931cf1a41ef2fe54b443444974c995ea1573c43ba83ccf1b701895bb134f025",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: T0>0 is required in the reservoir entropy relation."
        },
        {
