    "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. The map $\\sigma_\\alpha$ takes values in numerical state coordinates; its physical SI state map is explicitly $\\chi_\\alpha=\\Phi_c\\circ\\sigma_\\alpha$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the numerical-to-SI unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Thus the physical amounts at this point are $n_j=m x_j$, and the numerical amount coordinates in $\\sigma_\\alpha$ are $n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Derivative-defined physical state functions are evaluated on $\\chi_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, or equivalently pulled back along $\\Phi_c$ to $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$ in this precise pullback sense, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be\n$\\Sigma$-measurable. The nonnegative integral defined by\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] equals the shared nonnegative Lebesgue integral\ndefined by [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]], with the same value in\n$[0,+\\infty]$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f,g:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable and let $c\\ge0$.\n\n1. If $f\\le g$, then $\\int f\\,d\\mu\\le\\int g\\,d\\mu$.\n2. If $c>0$, then $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$. For $c=0$,\n   the integral of the zero function is $0$. Neither clause forms the\n   undefined extended-real product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$, then\n$$\\left|\\int f\\,d\\mu\\right|\\le\\int |f|\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be continuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then:\n\n1. If $(X,d_X)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]), the image\n   $f[X] \\subseteq Y$ is a compact subset of $Y$.\n2. More generally, if $K \\subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$, then $f[K]$ is\n   a compact subset of $Y$.\n\nNo choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $K \\subseteq X$ be a\ncompact subset ([[def-metric-compactness]]). Then $K$ is closed in $X$\n([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: both covers below are given by a rule, and the\nindexed form of [[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]] returns indices rather than\nsets.\n\n**The converse is false in general.** A closed and bounded subset of an arbitrary\nmetric space need not be compact\n([[fs-closed-and-bounded-implies-compact-in-every-metric-space]]); it is exactly\nin $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that the converse holds ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "The map $h$ is continuous, so [L2] makes $H$ compact. For every Borel probability $P$ on $K$, each coordinate of $h$ is bounded and Borel. Compactness and [L1] give boundedness. If $|f|\\le M$ for one such coordinate, [F3] gives $\\int_K|f|\\,dP\\le M P(K)=M$, so $f$ is integrable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "L1",
            "F3",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Every point of $C$ is a finite convex combination of points $h(x_i)$. If such a representation has $N>d+2$, the linear map $T:\\mathbb R^N\\to\\mathbb R^{d+2}$ defined by $T(c)=(\\sum_i c_i h(x_i),\\sum_i c_i)$ has a nonzero kernel vector $c$ by [L3], because the coordinate bases give domain dimension $N$ and codomain dimension $d+2$. Thus $\\sum_i c_i=0$ and $\\sum_i c_i h(x_i)=0$, so $c$ has both positive and negative coordinates. After dropping zero weights from the representation, set $t=\\min_{c_i>0}\\theta_i/c_i$ and replace each weight $\\theta_i$ by $\\theta_i-tc_i$. The new weights remain nonnegative, still sum to one, represent the same point, and at least one is zero. Repeating reduces every combination to at most $d+2$ terms. Hence $C$ is the image of $K^{d+2}\\times\\Delta_{d+2}$ under $\\Theta((x_i),(\\theta_i))=\\sum_{i=1}^{d+2}\\theta_i h(x_i)$, where $\\Delta_{d+2}:=\\{(\\theta_i):\\theta_i\\ge0,\\sum_i\\theta_i=1\\}$; shorter combinations are padded with zero weights and any fixed point of nonempty $K$. Each factor $K$ is closed and bounded by [L2], and $\\Delta_{d+2}$ is closed by continuity of the finite coordinate sum and its coordinate inequalities; it is bounded since $0\\le\\theta_i\\le1$. Their product is therefore closed and bounded in $\\mathbb R^{(d+1)(d+2)}$, so compact by [L2]. To check $\\Theta$ is continuous on this compact parameter set, fix a parameter $((x_i),(\\theta_i))$. Each coordinate $h_j$ is bounded by some $M$ on $K$ and is continuous at each of the finitely many $x_i$. For any $\\epsilon>0$, choose neighborhoods of the $x_i$ making $|h_j(x_i')-h_j(x_i)|<\\epsilon/(2N)$ for every output coordinate $j$, and require $|\\theta_i'-\\theta_i|<\\epsilon/(2N(M+1))$. Since $0\\le\\theta_i'\\le1$ and $|h_j(x_i)|\\le M$, each product difference $|\\theta_i'h_j(x_i')-\\theta_ih_j(x_i)|<\\epsilon/N$; summing over $i$ makes each output-coordinate difference smaller than $\\epsilon$. Thus $\\Theta$ is continuous. The continuous-image clause of [L2] makes its image $C$ compact, and [L2] also makes $C$ closed.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L3",
            "L2",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix any Borel probability $P$ on $K$ and let $y(P):=(\\int_K x\\,dP,\\int_Ks\\,dP)=\\int_Kh\\,dP$, with coordinatewise integration. For $\\epsilon>0$, compactness of $H$ gives a finite cover of $H$ by open Euclidean balls of radius $\\epsilon$. Their preimages under $h$ are Borel; disjointifying them in a fixed finite order gives a finite Borel partition $(A_i)$ of $K$. For every nonempty part choose $z_i\\in A_i$ and put $\\theta_i=P(A_i)$. Then $y_\\epsilon:=\\sum_i\\theta_i h(z_i)$ belongs to $C$. For $x\\in A_i$, both $h(x)$ and $h(z_i)$ lie in the same radius-$\\epsilon$ ball, so each coordinate difference is at most $2\\epsilon$. The simple map equal to $h(z_i)$ on $A_i$ has integral $y_\\epsilon$ by the simple-integral definition. By linearity and the integral triangle inequality in [F3], each coordinate of $y(P)-y_\\epsilon$ has absolute value at most $2\\epsilon\\sum_iP(A_i)=2\\epsilon$. Consequently $\\|y(P)-y_\\epsilon\\|_2\\le2\\epsilon\\sqrt{d+1}$, so the sequence obtained with $\\epsilon=1/n$ converges to $y(P)$. Since $C$ is closed by step 2.1, $y(P)\\in C$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "Because $\\bar x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, a finite convex combination of generators has barycenter $\\bar x$; its corresponding combination of points $h(x)$ shows that $E_{\\bar x}:=\\{r\\in\\mathbb R:(\\bar x,r)\\in C\\}$ is nonempty. The set $E_{\\bar x}$ is bounded because $C$ is bounded, and it is closed because $C$ is closed and $r\\mapsto(\\bar x,r)$ is continuous. By [L2] it is compact in $\\mathbb R$, so [L1] applied to the identity function yields a maximum $r_*\\in E_{\\bar x}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "L1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-5-1",
          "claim": "Represent $(\\bar x,r_*)\\in C$ by a finite convex combination $\\sum_{i=1}^N\\theta_i(x_i,s(x_i))$, dropping zero weights. If $N>d+1$, the linear map $R:\\mathbb R^N\\to\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ given by $R(c)=(\\sum_i c_ix_i,\\sum_i c_i)$ has a nonzero kernel vector by [L3], since its domain dimension is $N$ and its codomain dimension is $d+1$. Hence $\\sum_i c_i=0$ and $\\sum_i c_ix_i=0$, with both signs among the $c_i$. If $\\sum_i c_is(x_i)\\ne0$, choose the sign of $c$ so that this sum is positive. For sufficiently small $t>0$, the weights $\\theta_i+tc_i$ are nonnegative, preserve total mass and barycenter, and increase the last coordinate above $r_*$, contradicting maximality in step 4.1. Thus $\\sum_i c_is(x_i)=0$. Now take $t=\\min_{c_i>0}\\theta_i/c_i$ and replace $\\theta_i$ by $\\theta_i-tc_i$. This removes at least one positive weight while preserving total mass, barycenter, and entropy value $r_*$. Repeating finitely many times leaves at most $d+1$ positive weights.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L3",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-6-1",
          "claim": "Let $P_*:=\\sum_i\\theta_i\\delta_{x_i}$ be the probability measure from step 5.1. Each $\\delta_{x_i}$ is countably additive because among disjoint Borel sets at most one contains $x_i$, and $\\delta_{x_i}(K)=1$; hence $P_*$ is a Borel probability measure. It has barycenter $\\bar x$ and entropy $r_*$, so it is feasible by the model assumption. For any other feasible Borel probability $P$, step 3.1 gives $(\\bar x,\\int s\\,dP)\\in C$, so $\\int s\\,dP\\le r_*$. Thus $P_*$ attains the entropy maximum among all Borel mixtures and has at most $d+1$ atoms. No finite generator menu was assumed.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "K is explicitly nonempty and the barycenter belongs to conv K. Current proof4.1 constructs a feasible finite convex combination; proof6.1 supplies its Borel probability law. No maximum on an empty generator domain is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty compact set of dimensionless generator coordinates. Each $x\\in K$ labels one model-specified phase-generator state; the model assigns it entropy per mole $s(x)\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $s:K\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous, using the numerical entropy value in that fixed unit. Define $\\operatorname{conv}K$ to be the set of all finite convex combinations of points of $K$, and fix $\\bar x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$. Use the Borel sigma algebra relative to $K$. A Borel probability mixture is a countably additive probability measure $P$ on that sigma algebra; its barycenter and entropy per mole are the coordinatewise integrals $\\int_Kx\\,dP$ and $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. For $x\\in K$, define the unit point mass $\\delta_x$ by $\\delta_x(A)=1$ if $x\\in A$ and $\\delta_x(A)=0$ otherwise, for Borel $A\\subseteq K$. Assume every Borel probability mixture with barycenter $\\bar x$ is feasible and has entropy per mole $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. Then there are $m\\le d+1$, points $x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in K$, and weights $\\theta_i\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i=1$ such that $P_*:=\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i\\delta_{x_i}$ is a feasible Borel probability mixture and $\\int_Ks\\,dP_*\\ge\\int_Ks\\,dP$ for every feasible mixture $P$.\n\nHere integrals use the measure convention of [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. The entropy map is part of the model; this theorem derives neither its microscopic meaning nor compactness of a physical phase domain.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "902d766bdda0e9417e1e578e9ddc3a353f28773c0a6571490576d1a5cf32e475",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
