      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. The map $\\sigma_\\alpha$ takes values in numerical state coordinates; its physical SI state map is explicitly $\\chi_\\alpha=\\Phi_c\\circ\\sigma_\\alpha$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the numerical-to-SI unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Thus the physical amounts at this point are $n_j=m x_j$, and the numerical amount coordinates in $\\sigma_\\alpha$ are $n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Derivative-defined physical state functions are evaluated on $\\chi_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, or equivalently pulled back along $\\Phi_c$ to $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$ in this precise pullback sense, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current A1 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 6.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-borel-sigma-algebra.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F1 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1, 6.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-probability-measure",
          "carrier": "items/def-probability-measure.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F1 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1, 6.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-measure-integral.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F2 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be\n$\\Sigma$-measurable. The nonnegative integral defined by\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] equals the shared nonnegative Lebesgue integral\ndefined by [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]], with the same value in\n$[0,+\\infty]$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F2 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "carrier": "items/def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F3 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "carrier": "items/thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F3 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "carrier": "items/prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f,g:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable and let $c\\ge0$.\n\n1. If $f\\le g$, then $\\int f\\,d\\mu\\le\\int g\\,d\\mu$.\n2. If $c>0$, then $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$. For $c=0$,\n   the integral of the zero function is $0$. Neither clause forms the\n   undefined extended-real product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F3 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "carrier": "items/thm-integral-triangle-inequality.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "If $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$, then\n$$\\left|\\int f\\,d\\mu\\right|\\le\\int |f|\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current F3 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "carrier": "items/thm-extreme-value-metric.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current L1 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 2.1, 4.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "carrier": "items/thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be continuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then:\n\n1. If $(X,d_X)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]), the image\n   $f[X] \\subseteq Y$ is a compact subset of $Y$.\n2. More generally, if $K \\subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$, then $f[K]$ is\n   a compact subset of $Y$.\n\nNo choice principle is used.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current L2 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded",
          "carrier": "items/thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $K \\subseteq X$ be a\ncompact subset ([[def-metric-compactness]]). Then $K$ is closed in $X$\n([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: both covers below are given by a rule, and the\nindexed form of [[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]] returns indices rather than\nsets.\n\n**The converse is false in general.** A closed and bounded subset of an arbitrary\nmetric space need not be compact\n([[fs-closed-and-bounded-implies-compact-in-every-metric-space]]); it is exactly\nin $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that the converse holds ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current L2 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "carrier": "items/thm-heine-borel-rn.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current L2 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 1.1, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "carrier": "items/thm-rank-nullity.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Current L3 as declared in the source Facts & Assumptions; only the quoted interface is used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Existing published/draft interface; no new independent proof acceptance or source reading asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in current proof rows 2.1, 5.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "open",
        "reviewer": "batch-13-author self-audit",
        "notes": "The consuming proof is complete conditional on the exact registered supplier statement. Its current batch-12 proof has the exact gaps in the supplier excerpt and cross-batch ledger; this is not independent approval."
      }
    },
    "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n\u201cGlobal\u201d refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. The map $\\sigma_\\alpha$ takes values in numerical state coordinates; its physical SI state map is explicitly $\\chi_\\alpha=\\Phi_c\\circ\\sigma_\\alpha$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the numerical-to-SI unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Thus the physical amounts at this point are $n_j=m x_j$, and the numerical amount coordinates in $\\sigma_\\alpha$ are $n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Derivative-defined physical state functions are evaluated on $\\chi_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, or equivalently pulled back along $\\Phi_c$ to $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$ in this precise pullback sense, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite list of named subsystems $s_1,\\ldots,s_m$, where $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and $m\\ge1$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), each having its own equilibrium state domain $D_{c_i}$ as defined in [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Their separated endpoint state space is the Cartesian product ([[def-cartesian-product]])\n\n$$\\mathcal D:=D_{c_1}\\times\\cdots\\times D_{c_m}.$$\n\nAn **isolated compound** is the system consisting of those subsystems and any named auxiliaries or work devices included inside a declared outer boundary, with no matter or energy transfer across that outer boundary during the admitted process, in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]]. Transfers between constituents are internal to the compound. Its total energy and other extensive coordinates use the composition convention of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. The additive entropy sum applies only when the constituent split is admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] as a collection of independent separated macroscopic systems; an interacting compound outside that scope needs its own total-entropy model.\n\nFor specified totals and retained barriers, an **equilibrium feasible set** is the model-declared subset $\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal D$ of endpoint tuples satisfying those totals and exactly the exchange permissions in the declared protocol. Totals are imposed on the dimensioned coordinate maps $U_c,V_c,n_{c,j}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], not by treating the dimensionless numerical coordinates as quantities with units. For example, if two constituents are in thermal contact through [[def-td-thermal-contact-constraints]] while their volumes and mole amounts remain fixed, $\\mathcal F$ consists of the energy partitions $(x_A,x_B)$ with the prescribed total energy and those fixed volumes and amounts. Allowing volume or species exchange changes $\\mathcal F$ only when the protocol explicitly permits that exchange; retained walls keep the corresponding coordinates fixed.\n\nThe set $\\mathcal F$ may be empty, noncompact, or otherwise fail to contain an entropy-maximizing state. This definition imposes no existence, compactness, convexity, uniqueness, or attainment assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-bulk-additivity",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $A$ and $B$ be two named subsystems with disjoint physical regions as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], combined as a compound system $A\\sqcup B$ inside a declared outer boundary. The model's admitted separated pairs form a specified subset $\\mathcal C_{AB}\\subseteq D_A\\times D_B$ using [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]] and [[def-cartesian-product]]. For every pair $(x_A,x_B)\\in\\mathcal C_{AB}$, the composition map $\\kappa_{AB}:\\mathcal C_{AB}\\to D_{A\\sqcup B}$ is a function ([[def-function-space]]) adopted so the dimensioned coordinates of its output satisfy\n$$U_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=U_A(x_A)+U_B(x_B),\\qquad V_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=V_A(x_A)+V_B(x_B),\\qquad n_{A\\sqcup B,j}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=n_{A,j}(x_A)+n_{B,j}(x_B).$$\nHere each coordinate function is the dimensioned map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] for its material constitution, with SI units as in [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nHere \u201cextensive coordinates\u201d means precisely the named coordinates declared additive by this postulate. Amount coordinates are aligned by species label; when a species is absent from one subsystem, its zero amount is in a separately declared composition boundary, not the positive-mole chart of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This is an idealized composition rule: cross-interface and long-range interaction energy is omitted from $U_{A\\sqcup B}$, rather than asserted to vanish in nature. The postulate does not assert that every pair of subsystem states is physically admissible.\n\nThe framework's bulk family is a tagged family of material types. For each dimensionless scale $\\lambda>0$ the model may designate a tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and a subset $B_{c,\\lambda}\\subseteq D_c$ of states admitting a scaled copy, with inclusion as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$, the scaling map is the function $\\delta_\\lambda:B_{c,\\lambda}\\to D_{c^{(\\lambda)}}$ ([[def-function-space]]) given by $\\delta_\\lambda(x)=(\\lambda\\hat U,\\lambda\\hat V,\\lambda\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda\\hat n_r)$. The tag matters: $c^{(\\lambda)}$ is not silently identified with $c$. This postulate specifies the coordinate scaling model and energy additivity only; it does not postulate entropy additivity or exact scaling for every finite physical body.\n\nThis is an adopted physical composition rule, not a deduction from SI units or from the mathematics of Cartesian products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb\u2013Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$, with $r$ named species and dimensionless equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in D_c$, define the diagonal SI coordinate map\n\n$$\\Phi_c(x):=(U_c(x),V_c(x),n_{c,1}(x),\\ldots,n_{c,r}(x))=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n_1\\,\\mathrm{mol},\\ldots,\\hat n_r\\,\\mathrm{mol}).$$\n\nIn SI numerical coordinates this is a diagonal linear bijection with a diagonal linear inverse, so it maps ambient open sets to open sets and its inverse is defined on its image.\n\nChoose an open subset $O\\subseteq D_c$ in the ambient dimensionless Euclidean coordinates that is designated as a single-phase chart, and set $\\Omega:=\\Phi_c(O)$, an open chart of physical SI coordinate values. Here $U$ is the internal-energy coordinate evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. If $S_c:D_c\\to\\mathbb R$ is the entropy adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], define its physical-coordinate representative on $\\Omega$ by\n\n$$S_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R.$$\n\nWhen its arguments are the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, write this representative as $S_c(U,V,n)$ for brevity. On $\\Omega$, assume the finite representative is $C^2$ and has $S_{c,U}>0$, as adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in the physical $U$ coordinate while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; in particular the conversion factor from $\\hat U$ is included.\n\nExplicitly, with the partial derivatives on the right taken in the dimensionless coordinates on $O$ while holding every other hatted coordinate fixed, the physical partials are\n\n$$S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat U}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat V}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat n_j}S_c,$$\n\nusing the coordinate-conversion convention of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]].\n\nDefine the physical-coordinate representatives of the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}},\\qquad p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=-T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}.$$\n\nFor a state-domain point $x\\in O$, define the corresponding state functions by pullback, $T_c(x):=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, $p_c(x):=p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(x):=\\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$. When the argument is a physical tuple $(U,V,n)$, write $T_c(U,V,n)$, $p_c(U,V,n)$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(U,V,n)$ for the physical-coordinate representatives. Thus both notations refer to the same state functions through $\\Phi_c$.\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix any pair $\\alpha,\\beta$ and an allowed energy transfer. For any fixed nonzero increment $h$ joules, bilateral local feasibility supplies the curve $U_\\alpha(t)=U_\\alpha^*+th$, $U_\\beta(t)=U_\\beta^*-th$ for all sufficiently small $t$ of either sign, with all other coordinates fixed. The total entropy along this curve has a local maximum at $t=0$ by [A1]. Its derivative exists by [F2]; the right difference quotients are nonpositive and the left difference quotients are nonnegative, so the derivative is zero. By additivity and [L1], this derivative is $h(S_{\\alpha,U}-S_{\\beta,U})$. Since $h\\ne0$, $S_{\\alpha,U}=S_{\\beta,U}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], $S_{\\alpha,U}=1/T_\\alpha$ and $S_{\\beta,U}=1/T_\\beta$. Both temperatures are finite and positive, so equality of the reciprocals gives $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If volume transfer is permitted, take a nonzero increment $k$ in $\\mathrm{m^3}$ and vary $V_\\alpha(t)=V_\\alpha^*+tk$, $V_\\beta(t)=V_\\beta^*-tk$ with both signs feasible. The same local-maximum quotient argument gives $k(S_{\\alpha,V}-S_{\\beta,V})=0$, hence $S_{\\alpha,V}=S_{\\beta,V}$. By [F3], $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$. When energy transfer is also permitted, step 2.1 gives equal positive temperatures, so $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$. These are derivative pressures; a mechanical piston interpretation requires its separately declared coupling.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "If species $j$ is transferable, vary its mole amounts by $n_{\\alpha,j}(t)=n_{\\alpha,j}^*+tq$ and $n_{\\beta,j}(t)=n_{\\beta,j}^*-tq$ for a nonzero increment $q$ in moles. Both signs are feasible. The same quotient argument gives $q(S_{\\alpha,n_j}-S_{\\beta,n_j})=0$, hence equal entropy partials. By [F3], $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$. If energy transfer is also permitted, step 2.1 gives equal positive temperatures and therefore equal chemical potentials.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Every pair was arbitrary, so the asserted pairwise equalities hold for all named phases and only for the channels whose bilateral feasibility was assumed. If $P=1$, there are no pairs and the conditions are vacuous. No derivative equality follows for a retained or boundary coordinate because its required two-sided feasible curve is absent.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 4.1: P=1 has no phase pairs."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "zero: The zero boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "a100f2c573cb68bdc22ce4c7fe35f0555a74ace99b706c4ae40624d229974e86",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
