            "source_excerpt": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
