      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-entropy-maximum.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n\u201cGlobal\u201d refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[post-td-entropy-maximum]]: A stable endpoint attains a global maximum of total entropy on its exact feasible endpoint set; local maxima alone are not called stable equilibrium.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]: Phase amounts and states lie in the declared branch charts and exact feasible set; only the listed totals and exchanges may vary.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a finite list of named subsystems $s_1,\\ldots,s_m$, where $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and $m\\ge1$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), each having its own equilibrium state domain $D_{c_i}$ as defined in [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Their separated endpoint state space is the Cartesian product ([[def-cartesian-product]])\n\n$$\\mathcal D:=D_{c_1}\\times\\cdots\\times D_{c_m}.$$\n\nAn **isolated compound** is the system consisting of those subsystems and any named auxiliaries or work devices included inside a declared outer boundary, with no matter or energy transfer across that outer boundary during the admitted process, in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]]. Transfers between constituents are internal to the compound. Its total energy and other extensive coordinates use the composition convention of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. The additive entropy sum applies only when the constituent split is admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] as a collection of independent separated macroscopic systems; an interacting compound outside that scope needs its own total-entropy model.\n\nFor specified totals and retained barriers, an **equilibrium feasible set** is the model-declared subset $\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal D$ of endpoint tuples satisfying those totals and exactly the exchange permissions in the declared protocol. Totals are imposed on the dimensioned coordinate maps $U_c,V_c,n_{c,j}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], not by treating the dimensionless numerical coordinates as quantities with units. For example, if two constituents are in thermal contact through [[def-td-thermal-contact-constraints]] while their volumes and mole amounts remain fixed, $\\mathcal F$ consists of the energy partitions $(x_A,x_B)$ with the prescribed total energy and those fixed volumes and amounts. Allowing volume or species exchange changes $\\mathcal F$ only when the protocol explicitly permits that exchange; retained walls keep the corresponding coordinates fixed.\n\nThe set $\\mathcal F$ may be empty, noncompact, or otherwise fail to contain an entropy-maximizing state. This definition imposes no existence, compactness, convexity, uniqueness, or attainment assumption.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]: Phase amounts and states lie in the declared branch charts and exact feasible set; only the listed totals and exchanges may vary.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-bulk-additivity",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-bulk-additivity.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A$ and $B$ be two named subsystems with disjoint physical regions as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], combined as a compound system $A\\sqcup B$ inside a declared outer boundary. The model's admitted separated pairs form a specified subset $\\mathcal C_{AB}\\subseteq D_A\\times D_B$ using [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]] and [[def-cartesian-product]]. For every pair $(x_A,x_B)\\in\\mathcal C_{AB}$, the composition map $\\kappa_{AB}:\\mathcal C_{AB}\\to D_{A\\sqcup B}$ is a function ([[def-function-space]]) adopted so the dimensioned coordinates of its output satisfy\n$$U_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=U_A(x_A)+U_B(x_B),\\qquad V_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=V_A(x_A)+V_B(x_B),\\qquad n_{A\\sqcup B,j}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=n_{A,j}(x_A)+n_{B,j}(x_B).$$\nHere each coordinate function is the dimensioned map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] for its material constitution, with SI units as in [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nHere \u201cextensive coordinates\u201d means precisely the named coordinates declared additive by this postulate. Amount coordinates are aligned by species label; when a species is absent from one subsystem, its zero amount is in a separately declared composition boundary, not the positive-mole chart of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This is an idealized composition rule: cross-interface and long-range interaction energy is omitted from $U_{A\\sqcup B}$, rather than asserted to vanish in nature. The postulate does not assert that every pair of subsystem states is physically admissible.\n\nThe framework's bulk family is a tagged family of material types. For each dimensionless scale $\\lambda>0$ the model may designate a tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and a subset $B_{c,\\lambda}\\subseteq D_c$ of states admitting a scaled copy, with inclusion as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$, the scaling map is the function $\\delta_\\lambda:B_{c,\\lambda}\\to D_{c^{(\\lambda)}}$ ([[def-function-space]]) given by $\\delta_\\lambda(x)=(\\lambda\\hat U,\\lambda\\hat V,\\lambda\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda\\hat n_r)$. The tag matters: $c^{(\\lambda)}$ is not silently identified with $c$. This postulate specifies the coordinate scaling model and energy additivity only; it does not postulate entropy additivity or exact scaling for every finite physical body.\n\nThis is an adopted physical composition rule, not a deduction from SI units or from the mathematics of Cartesian products.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]: For the specified separated compound, energy, volume, aligned species amounts, and entropy add; on each selected single-phase chart the entropy is finite and C2 with $S_U>0$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb\u2013Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]: For the specified separated compound, energy, volume, aligned species amounts, and entropy add; on each selected single-phase chart the entropy is finite and C2 with $S_U>0$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]: $S_U=1/T$, $S_V=p/T$, and $S_{n_j}=-\\mu_j/T$, each partial holding the other physical coordinates fixed.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2, 2.1, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "carrier": "items/thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives.md",
          "status": "current published supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]: The derivative of a differentiable composition is the composition of its derivatives.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-generated; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1, 3.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "batch-13-author self-audit",
        "notes": "Current numbered steps, exact linked interfaces, units, physical assumptions, and applicable boundaries/finite calculations were checked by the author. This is not independent approval."
      }
    },
    "pthm-td-reaction-boundary-equilibrium": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a system at a positive temperature $T_0$ and external pressure $p_0>0$. Internal reaction extents may vary in a declared feasible set, but no species crosses the system's outer boundary. The system contacts only an ideal thermal reservoir at $T_0$ and an ideal pressure store at $p_0$; all other work coordinates and excluded-energy changes are absent. Let $U$ be system internal energy in joules, $V$ volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, and $S$ system entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. Define the constrained potential\n$$\n\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}:=U+p_0V-T_0S.\n$$\nFor every admitted spontaneous endpoint change, $\\Delta\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}\\le0$. If the system-plus-reservoir-plus-store endpoint is an attained global entropy maximum on its exact feasible set, then the corresponding system endpoint attains a global minimum of $\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}$ over the induced feasible reaction states. This is a conditional model deduction; it does not assert that arbitrary fixed-$T,p$ reactions have this preparation or that the system reaches the minimum dynamically.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-td-potential-differentials",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On a valid local energy-representation chart from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]], let $s,V,n_j,U,T,p,\\mu_j$ and the potentials $H,F,G,\\Omega$ be as defined in [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. On every open neighborhood where the corresponding natural-coordinate maps have their stated $C^1$ inverses, the potentials are C2 and their differentials are\n\n$$dU=T\\,ds-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dH=T\\,ds+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$dF=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dG=-s\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$d\\Omega=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV-\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j.$$\n\nAll differentials are local state-function differentials. They do not assert that heat or work is an exact differential on process space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s,r\\ge1$, let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{s\\times r}$ be a stoichiometric matrix (column $k$ gives the signed species-amount change per unit extent of reaction $k$), and let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^s$ be initial species amounts. Extents $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r$ are measured in one fixed amount unit, so $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$ has species amounts in that same unit; $\\nu$ is dimensionless. The feasible extent set is the polyhedron\n\n$$\\mathcal F=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^s\\}.$$\n\nAt $\\xi_*\\in\\mathcal F$, let $J=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. Define the Bouligand tangent cone $T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ to consist of all $h\\in\\mathbb R^r$ for which there are $t_k>0$ with $t_k\\to0$ and $h_k\\to h$ such that $\\xi_*+t_kh_k\\in\\mathcal F$. Then\n\n$$T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^r:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J\\}.$$\n\nIf $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ is open with $\\xi_*\\in U$, and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ is differentiable with a local minimum at $\\xi_*$ relative to $\\mathcal F\\cap U$, then $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$ for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$. Conversely, if $U$ is convex and $f$ is convex and differentiable on $U$, this directional inequality for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ implies $f(\\xi)\\ge f(\\xi_*)$ for every $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$. If a potential $\\Phi$ is differentiable on an open neighborhood of the relevant amounts and $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi)$, define $\\mu_j=\\partial\\Phi/\\partial n_j$; then\n\n$$Df(\\xi_*)h=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h.$$\n\nThe tangent-cone and optimality claims are mathematical deductions. They do not assert that a particular physical system obeys this model or is at equilibrium.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $\\xi_*$ is a local minimum of $\\Phi$ relative to $\\mathcal F_\\nu\\cap U$. At positive-amount points, the chemical-potential derivative in [A1] agrees with the Gibbs differential supplied by [F2]; at zero amounts it is the separately assumed extension. Hence [A1] gives the differentiable ambient derivative $(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*))\\cdot h$ at the point under consideration.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] at $\\xi_*$. It identifies the feasible tangent directions as exactly the vectors satisfying $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for all $j\\in J_*$, and the local-minimum inequality gives $(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*))\\cdot h\\ge0$ for every such direction.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $J_*=\\varnothing$, then every small direction $h\\in\\mathbb R^R$ is feasible, so the tangent cone is all of $\\mathbb R^R$. Put $a:=\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*)$. The inequality holds for $h=-a$; it gives $-|a|^2\\ge0$, hence $a=0$. This is the interior affinity condition.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "If $\\Phi$ is convex and differentiable on a convex domain containing the relevant feasible set, the sufficiency clause of [F3] makes the tangent inequality sufficient for global minimization on that domain. Without convexity this sufficiency conclusion is not asserted.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 is the empty-active-set interior case."
