          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $C\\ge1$ species and $R\\ge1$ declared internal reaction extents. Let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{C\\times R}$ be the dimensionless stoichiometric matrix, let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^C$ have units mol, and let $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R$ have units mol. The amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$, and the nonnegative-amount feasible extent set is\n$$\n\\mathcal F_\\nu:=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^C\\}.\n$$\nFix $T_0>0$ and $p_0$ in a declared natural-variable chart. Let $\\Phi(\\xi)$ be the constrained Gibbs potential on an open neighborhood $U$ of an equilibrium extent $\\xi_*$, and assume the model supplies a finite $C^1$ extension through every zero-amount boundary point under consideration. On $U$, assume\n$$\nD\\Phi(\\xi)[h]=\\sum_{j=1}^C\\mu_j(\\xi)(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi))\\cdot h,\n$$\nwhere $\\mu_j$ are the molar chemical potentials in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. This extension and derivative identity are not supplied by the positive-mole phase chart itself.\n\nSuppose $\\xi_*$ is an attained stable equilibrium for the fixed-$T_0,p_0$ internal reaction model with the ideal reservoirs of [[pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum]]. Then $\\xi_*$ minimizes $\\Phi$ on the exact reaction-feasible set locally. Let $J_*:=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. The feasible tangent cone is\n$$\nT_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*):=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^R:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J_*\\}.\n$$\nThe first-order necessary condition is\n$$\n(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*))\\cdot h\\ge0\\qquad(h\\in T_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*)).\n$$\nIf $\\xi_*$ is interior to $\\mathcal F_\\nu$, this reduces to $\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*)=0$. If, in addition, $\\Phi$ is convex and differentiable on a convex neighborhood containing the relevant feasible set, the same tangent inequality is sufficient for global minimization there.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "6c680fd8f74e432bff7f2bf1bc3aac7abf495c111dd71c82b6e7e0cc849071e0",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Fix $C\\ge1$ species and $R\\ge1$ declared internal reaction extents. Boundary iff-reverse: The iff-reverse boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $C\\ge1$ species and $R\\ge1$ declared internal reaction extents. Let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{C\\times R}$ be the dimensionless stoichiometric matrix, let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^C$ have units mol, and let $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R$ have units mol. The amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$, and the nonnegative-amount feasible extent set is\n$$\n\\mathcal F_\\nu:=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^C\\}.\n$$\nFix $T_0>0$ and $p_0$ in a declared natural-variable chart. Let $\\Phi(\\xi)$ be the constrained Gibbs potential on an open neighborhood $U$ of an equilibrium extent $\\xi_*$, and assume the model supplies a finite $C^1$ extension through every zero-amount boundary point under consideration. On $U$, assume\n$$\nD\\Phi(\\xi)[h]=\\sum_{j=1}^C\\mu_j(\\xi)(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi))\\cdot h,\n$$\nwhere $\\mu_j$ are the molar chemical potentials in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. This extension and derivative identity are not supplied by the positive-mole phase chart itself.\n\nSuppose $\\xi_*$ is an attained stable equilibrium for the fixed-$T_0,p_0$ internal reaction model with the ideal reservoirs of [[pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum]]. Then $\\xi_*$ minimizes $\\Phi$ on the exact reaction-feasible set locally. Let $J_*:=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. The feasible tangent cone is\n$$\nT_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*):=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^R:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J_*\\}.\n$$\nThe first-order necessary condition is\n$$\n(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*))\\cdot h\\ge0\\qquad(h\\in T_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*)).\n$$\nIf $\\xi_*$ is interior to $\\mathcal F_\\nu$, this reduces to $\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*)=0$. If, in addition, $\\Phi$ is convex and differentiable on a convex neighborhood containing the relevant feasible set, the same tangent inequality is sufficient for global minimization there.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "6c680fd8f74e432bff7f2bf1bc3aac7abf495c111dd71c82b6e7e0cc849071e0",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a system at a positive temperature $T_0$ and external pressure $p_0>0$. Internal reaction extents may vary in a declared feasible set, but no species crosses the system's outer boundary. The system contacts only an ideal thermal reservoir at $T_0$ and an ideal pressure store at $p_0$; all other work coordinates and excluded-energy changes are absent. Let $U$ be system internal energy in joules, $V$ volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, and $S$ system entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. Define the constrained potential\n\\[\n\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}:=U+p_0V-T_0S.\n\\]\nFor every admitted spontaneous endpoint change, $\\Delta\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}\\le0$. If the system-plus-reservoir-plus-store endpoint is an attained global entropy maximum on its exact feasible set, then the corresponding system endpoint attains a global minimum of $\\Phi_{T_0,p_0}$ over the induced feasible reaction states. This is a conditional model deduction; it does not assert that arbitrary fixed-$T,p$ reactions have this preparation or that the system reaches the minimum dynamically.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum]]: Under the explicitly stated ideal-bath, ideal-pressure-store and isolated-compound assumptions, an attained global entropy maximum yields a global minimum of $U+p_0V-T_0S$ on the internal reaction-feasible endpoint set. On the valid fixed-$(T_0,p_0)$ Gibbs chart this is $\\Phi$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-potential-differentials",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-potential-differentials.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "On a valid local energy-representation chart from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]], let $s,V,n_j,U,T,p,\\mu_j$ and the potentials $H,F,G,\\Omega$ be as defined in [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. On every open neighborhood where the corresponding natural-coordinate maps have their stated $C^1$ inverses, the potentials are C2 and their differentials are\n\n$$dU=T\\,ds-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dH=T\\,ds+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$dF=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dG=-s\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$d\\Omega=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV-\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j.$$\n\nAll differentials are local state-function differentials. They do not assert that heat or work is an exact differential on process space.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-potential-differentials]]: On a valid single-phase energy-representation and Gibbs natural-variable chart, $dG=-S\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_j\\mu_j\\,dn_j$. The current carrier is registered in batch 9 and has draft status; its proof is conditional on the actual local-energy and partial-Legendre suppliers, whose inspected interfaces and proof statuses are recorded in batch-13 cross-batch evidence.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-9 draft; the differential statement matches the use, conditional on its actual local-energy and partial-Legendre suppliers.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $s,r\\ge1$, let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{s\\times r}$ be a stoichiometric matrix (column $k$ gives the signed species-amount change per unit extent of reaction $k$), and let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^s$ be initial species amounts. Extents $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r$ are measured in one fixed amount unit, so $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$ has species amounts in that same unit; $\\nu$ is dimensionless. The feasible extent set is the polyhedron\n\n$$\\mathcal F=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^s\\}.$$\n\nAt $\\xi_*\\in\\mathcal F$, let $J=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. Define the Bouligand tangent cone $T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ to consist of all $h\\in\\mathbb R^r$ for which there are $t_k>0$ with $t_k\\to0$ and $h_k\\to h$ such that $\\xi_*+t_kh_k\\in\\mathcal F$. Then\n\n$$T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^r:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J\\}.$$\n\nIf $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ is open with $\\xi_*\\in U$, and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ is differentiable with a local minimum at $\\xi_*$ relative to $\\mathcal F\\cap U$, then $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$ for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$. Conversely, if $U$ is convex and $f$ is convex and differentiable on $U$, this directional inequality for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ implies $f(\\xi)\\ge f(\\xi_*)$ for every $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$. If a potential $\\Phi$ is differentiable on an open neighborhood of the relevant amounts and $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi)$, define $\\mu_j=\\partial\\Phi/\\partial n_j$; then\n\n$$Df(\\xi_*)h=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h.$$\n\nThe tangent-cone and optimality claims are mathematical deductions. They do not assert that a particular physical system obeys this model or is at equilibrium.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone]]: For the nonnegative stoichiometric feasible polyhedron, the tangent cone consists exactly of $h$ with $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for every active species. At a differentiable local minimum, the directional derivative is nonnegative on that cone; for a differentiable convex objective on a convex domain, the same condition is sufficient for a global minimum.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-4 draft; its active-species tangent-cone interface matches the consumer. Proof remains draft, not independent acceptance.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2, 3.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "batch-13-author self-audit",
        "notes": "Current numbered steps, exact linked interfaces, units, physical assumptions, and applicable boundaries/finite calculations were checked by the author. This is not independent approval."
      }
    },
    "pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. The map $\\sigma_\\alpha$ takes values in numerical state coordinates; its physical SI state map is explicitly $\\chi_\\alpha=\\Phi_c\\circ\\sigma_\\alpha$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the numerical-to-SI unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Thus the physical amounts at this point are $n_j=m x_j$, and the numerical amount coordinates in $\\sigma_\\alpha$ are $n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Derivative-defined physical state functions are evaluated on $\\chi_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, or equivalently pulled back along $\\Phi_c$ to $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$ in this precise pullback sense, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n\u201cGlobal\u201d refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-bulk-additivity",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $A$ and $B$ be two named subsystems with disjoint physical regions as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], combined as a compound system $A\\sqcup B$ inside a declared outer boundary. The model's admitted separated pairs form a specified subset $\\mathcal C_{AB}\\subseteq D_A\\times D_B$ using [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]] and [[def-cartesian-product]]. For every pair $(x_A,x_B)\\in\\mathcal C_{AB}$, the composition map $\\kappa_{AB}:\\mathcal C_{AB}\\to D_{A\\sqcup B}$ is a function ([[def-function-space]]) adopted so the dimensioned coordinates of its output satisfy\n$$U_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=U_A(x_A)+U_B(x_B),\\qquad V_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=V_A(x_A)+V_B(x_B),\\qquad n_{A\\sqcup B,j}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=n_{A,j}(x_A)+n_{B,j}(x_B).$$\nHere each coordinate function is the dimensioned map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] for its material constitution, with SI units as in [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nHere \u201cextensive coordinates\u201d means precisely the named coordinates declared additive by this postulate. Amount coordinates are aligned by species label; when a species is absent from one subsystem, its zero amount is in a separately declared composition boundary, not the positive-mole chart of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This is an idealized composition rule: cross-interface and long-range interaction energy is omitted from $U_{A\\sqcup B}$, rather than asserted to vanish in nature. The postulate does not assert that every pair of subsystem states is physically admissible.\n\nThe framework's bulk family is a tagged family of material types. For each dimensionless scale $\\lambda>0$ the model may designate a tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and a subset $B_{c,\\lambda}\\subseteq D_c$ of states admitting a scaled copy, with inclusion as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$, the scaling map is the function $\\delta_\\lambda:B_{c,\\lambda}\\to D_{c^{(\\lambda)}}$ ([[def-function-space]]) given by $\\delta_\\lambda(x)=(\\lambda\\hat U,\\lambda\\hat V,\\lambda\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda\\hat n_r)$. The tag matters: $c^{(\\lambda)}$ is not silently identified with $c$. This postulate specifies the coordinate scaling model and energy additivity only; it does not postulate entropy additivity or exact scaling for every finite physical body.\n\nThis is an adopted physical composition rule, not a deduction from SI units or from the mathematics of Cartesian products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$, with $r$ named species and dimensionless equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in D_c$, define the diagonal SI coordinate map\n\n$$\\Phi_c(x):=(U_c(x),V_c(x),n_{c,1}(x),\\ldots,n_{c,r}(x))=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n_1\\,\\mathrm{mol},\\ldots,\\hat n_r\\,\\mathrm{mol}).$$\n\nIn SI numerical coordinates this is a diagonal linear bijection with a diagonal linear inverse, so it maps ambient open sets to open sets and its inverse is defined on its image.\n\nChoose an open subset $O\\subseteq D_c$ in the ambient dimensionless Euclidean coordinates that is designated as a single-phase chart, and set $\\Omega:=\\Phi_c(O)$, an open chart of physical SI coordinate values. Here $U$ is the internal-energy coordinate evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. If $S_c:D_c\\to\\mathbb R$ is the entropy adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], define its physical-coordinate representative on $\\Omega$ by\n\n$$S_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R.$$\n\nWhen its arguments are the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, write this representative as $S_c(U,V,n)$ for brevity. On $\\Omega$, assume the finite representative is $C^2$ and has $S_{c,U}>0$, as adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in the physical $U$ coordinate while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; in particular the conversion factor from $\\hat U$ is included.\n\nExplicitly, with the partial derivatives on the right taken in the dimensionless coordinates on $O$ while holding every other hatted coordinate fixed, the physical partials are\n\n$$S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat U}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat V}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat n_j}S_c,$$\n\nusing the coordinate-conversion convention of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]].\n\nDefine the physical-coordinate representatives of the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}},\\qquad p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=-T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}.$$\n\nFor a state-domain point $x\\in O$, define the corresponding state functions by pullback, $T_c(x):=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, $p_c(x):=p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(x):=\\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$. When the argument is a physical tuple $(U,V,n)$, write $T_c(U,V,n)$, $p_c(U,V,n)$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(U,V,n)$ for the physical-coordinate representatives. Thus both notations refer to the same state functions through $\\Phi_c$.\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here \u201clocal graph dimension $k$\u201d means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $P\\ge2$, define the phase-equality components $F_{i,\\alpha}:=\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\mu_i^1$ for $1\\le i\\le C$, $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$. There are exactly $q=C(P-1)$ such scalar equations. By [A1], each $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ is C1 on the common open domain, so their finite differences are C1. By [F1], these equations hold at the stated physical coexistence point when the corresponding species exchanges are admitted; [A2] supplies the entropy regularity and positive temperatures required by that supplier.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "A2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix any phase $\\alpha$ and reaction direction $\\nu^r$. Vary only that phase's species amounts as $n^\\alpha(t)=n^{\\alpha,*}+t\\nu^r$, holding its energy and volume and all other phase coordinates fixed; $t$ is a small extent in moles. By the stated bilateral feasibility, this curve is admitted for both signs of sufficiently small $t$. The total entropy has a local maximum at $t=0$ by [F2]. Its differentiability there follows from [A2]. The right difference quotients are nonpositive and the left difference quotients are nonnegative; the derivative is therefore zero. Additivity and [F3] give\n$$\n0=\\frac{dS_{\\mathrm{tot}}}{dt}(0)=\\sum_j S_{\\alpha,n_j}\\nu_j^r=-\\frac{(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha}{T}.\n$$\nSince $T>0$, $(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$ in every phase. The equations are C1 because they are finite linear combinations of the C1 branch maps. By [F1], phase equalities make the affinity equation identical across phases, so count only the $R$ equations in phase 1. The combined map therefore has $q+R$ equations.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-3",
          "claim": "The ambient coordinate dimension is $n=2+P(C-1)$. If $q+R=0$, then $P=1$ and $R=0$, so there are no phase-equality or reaction-affinity equations. The local zero set is all of the open chart and has dimension $n=2+(C-1)=C-P+2-R$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": []
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $q+R\\ge1$, apply [L1] on the stated open chart $\\mathcal U=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times(\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ})^P$ to the combined C1 equation map using the stated invertible minor. The local graph theorem applies on this full chart, including all real pressures in the statement. Its local zero set has dimension\n$$\nn-q-R=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)-R=C-P+2-R.\n$$\nThis conclusion is conditional on the full-rank minor; it does not follow from equation counting alone. Additional stability constraints may reduce the physical coexistence set.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 treats q+R=0 as the whole open chart."
