        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "iff-reverse: The iff-reverse boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the entropy of each phase is $C^2$ on its physical state chart and its derivative-defined temperature at the endpoint is finite and positive. The equation-set dimension below concerns this smooth interior state only.\n\nLet $C\\ge1$ be the number of named species, $P\\ge1$ the number of coexisting phase branches, and $R\\ge0$ the number of independent internal reaction directions. Consider an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For each phase $\\alpha$, use an intensive phase branch from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and an interior composition $x^\\alpha\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}$. Let each branch chemical-potential map\n$$\n\\mu^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)=(\\mu_1^\\alpha,\\ldots,\\mu_C^\\alpha)\n$$\nbe $C^1$ on a common open domain\n$$\n\\mathcal U=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times(\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ})^P \\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\,n},\\qquad n=2+P(C-1).\n$$\nAssume energy, volume, and every named species can be exchanged between every phase pair in both directions, and every listed reaction direction is feasible in both extent directions in every phase while that phase's energy, volume, other reaction extents, and all other phase coordinates are held fixed at the coexistence point. Let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent, dimensionless stoichiometric vectors, with extents measured in moles. Phase equilibrium imposes $\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\mu_i^1=0$ for $1\\le i\\le C$, $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$; internal reaction equilibrium imposes $(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$ in every phase for $1\\le r\\le R$. On the phase-equality locus these are the same $R$ equations in each phase, so count them once using phase 1. Put $q:=C(P-1)$ and $m:=q+R$. If $m=0$ (equivalently $P=1,R=0$), no equation is imposed and the local zero set is the whole chart, of dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2-R$. If $m\\ge1$ and the combined equation map has an invertible $m\\times m$ derivative minor at the solution, with $m\\le n$, then the local zero set of these first-order coexistence-and-affinity equations is a graph of dimension\n$$\nn-q-R=C-P+2-R.\n$$\nThis is the dimension of the equation zero set in intensive coordinates only; phase amounts and their extensive conservation constraints are not counted, and the actual stable-coexistence set may be a proper subset after additional stability constraints. If the nonsingular-minor condition fails, no dimension formula is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f68a20ba4a3bfda5a19a4c62cc374a12685f6d297a745f87e06af0ed78fbff05",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
