      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]]: Each branch is a model-supplied C1 intensive phase chart, so derivative-defined chemical potentials are independent of the phase amount and are C1 maps of $(T,p,x^\\alpha)$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions]]: At an attained entropy-maximizing coexistence point, bilateral energy and volume exchange imply equal positive temperature and pressure, and bilateral species exchange implies equal derivative-defined chemical potentials across phases.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-entropy-maximum.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n\u201cGlobal\u201d refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[post-td-entropy-maximum]] and [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]: The total entropy of the separated phases is the sum of their state entropies, and the adopted stable endpoint is a global maximum on its exact feasible set.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-bulk-additivity",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-bulk-additivity.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A$ and $B$ be two named subsystems with disjoint physical regions as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], combined as a compound system $A\\sqcup B$ inside a declared outer boundary. The model's admitted separated pairs form a specified subset $\\mathcal C_{AB}\\subseteq D_A\\times D_B$ using [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]] and [[def-cartesian-product]]. For every pair $(x_A,x_B)\\in\\mathcal C_{AB}$, the composition map $\\kappa_{AB}:\\mathcal C_{AB}\\to D_{A\\sqcup B}$ is a function ([[def-function-space]]) adopted so the dimensioned coordinates of its output satisfy\n$$U_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=U_A(x_A)+U_B(x_B),\\qquad V_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=V_A(x_A)+V_B(x_B),\\qquad n_{A\\sqcup B,j}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=n_{A,j}(x_A)+n_{B,j}(x_B).$$\nHere each coordinate function is the dimensioned map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] for its material constitution, with SI units as in [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nHere \u201cextensive coordinates\u201d means precisely the named coordinates declared additive by this postulate. Amount coordinates are aligned by species label; when a species is absent from one subsystem, its zero amount is in a separately declared composition boundary, not the positive-mole chart of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This is an idealized composition rule: cross-interface and long-range interaction energy is omitted from $U_{A\\sqcup B}$, rather than asserted to vanish in nature. The postulate does not assert that every pair of subsystem states is physically admissible.\n\nThe framework's bulk family is a tagged family of material types. For each dimensionless scale $\\lambda>0$ the model may designate a tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and a subset $B_{c,\\lambda}\\subseteq D_c$ of states admitting a scaled copy, with inclusion as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$, the scaling map is the function $\\delta_\\lambda:B_{c,\\lambda}\\to D_{c^{(\\lambda)}}$ ([[def-function-space]]) given by $\\delta_\\lambda(x)=(\\lambda\\hat U,\\lambda\\hat V,\\lambda\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda\\hat n_r)$. The tag matters: $c^{(\\lambda)}$ is not silently identified with $c$. This postulate specifies the coordinate scaling model and energy additivity only; it does not postulate entropy additivity or exact scaling for every finite physical body.\n\nThis is an adopted physical composition rule, not a deduction from SI units or from the mathematics of Cartesian products.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[post-td-entropy-maximum]] and [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]: The total entropy of the separated phases is the sum of their state entropies, and the adopted stable endpoint is a global maximum on its exact feasible set.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]: On each single-phase chart, $S_{n_j}=-\\mu_j/T$ at fixed energy, volume, and other species amounts; branch temperatures are positive.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-phase-dimension.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-tdmath-phase-dimension]]: For C1 chemical-potential maps on the stated open intensive composition chart, the zero set of the phase-equality equations and $R$ independent affinity equations has local dimension $2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)-R$ when the combined equation map has an invertible square derivative minor of size $C(P-1)+R$. The current batch-4 carrier and its proof contract are registered; the item remains a draft and is not independently accepted.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-4 draft; its C1 equation-zero-set dimension interface matches. Proof remains draft, not independent acceptance.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 2.2."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "batch-13-author self-audit",
        "notes": "Current numbered steps, exact linked interfaces, units, physical assumptions, and applicable boundaries/finite calculations were checked by the author. This is not independent approval."
      }
    },
    "pthm-td-clapeyron-local": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C$ be the open positive-temperature domain of a local C2 energy representation $\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ for a fixed material constitution $c$, with $V>0$, $n_j>0$, and conjugates $T=\\mathcal U_{c,s}$, $p=-\\mathcal U_{c,V}$, and $\\mu_j=\\mathcal U_{c,n_j}$. Let $W\\subseteq C$ be open. In addition to the framework suppliers, assume the **same-tag homogeneity hypothesis**: for every $q=(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in W$ there is an $\\varepsilon_q\\in(0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda n_r)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$$\n\nfor every $\\lambda>0$ with $|\\lambda-1|<\\varepsilon_q$ and $\\lambda q\\in C$. Then, throughout $W$,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c=Ts-pV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j,$$\n\nand on the same chart\n\n$$s\\,dT-V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j=0.$$\n\nThe same-tag hypothesis is supplied, in particular, when the inverse states $\\Psi(q):=(\\mathcal U_c(q),V,n)$ for $q\\in W$ lie in the designated common-law cone of [[post-td-common-bulk-constitutive-law]], expressed in physical SI coordinate values using the fixed unit conversion. On this branch the adopted entropy transport implies the energy homogeneity locally by uniqueness of the local inverse; no global inverse or global energy-domain cone is assumed. These are conditional model deductions. The general tagged relation in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] alone does not identify the scaled system with one fixed constitutive law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-td-reversible-process",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P=(I_P,x_P^-,x_P^+,z_P,\\kappa_P,r_P)$ be an admitted closed process record as defined in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$. The record is **reversible relative to its declared boundary and protocol** when the model also admits a reverse record $P^{\\mathrm{rev}}$ for the same system and material constitution, starting at $x_P^+$ and ending at $x_P^-$, such that every auxiliary and device state specified by the protocol is restored to its initial state and every signed boundary transfer is reversed. In particular,\n\n$$Q[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-Q[P],\\qquad W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P].$$\n\nIf the protocol records additional transfer channels, their signed values must likewise be negatives of their forward values. A record that is slow is not reversible merely because it is slow; it must satisfy the reverse-record condition above. Reversibility is therefore relative to what the model includes inside the boundary and which reverse controls it admits.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound. Fix the finite constituent split $s_1,\\ldots,s_m$ used for the endpoint bookkeeping, with $m\\in\\mathbb N$, $m\\ge1$, and constitutions $c_1,\\ldots,c_m$. The split accounts for every constituent whose entropy can change; any included auxiliary not represented in it must have an explicitly model-declared zero entropy change between these endpoints. Its endpoint product is\n\n$$\\mathcal D:=D_{c_1}\\times\\cdots\\times D_{c_m},\\qquad \\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal D,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal F$ is the model-declared feasible endpoint set for the fixed totals, retained barriers, and permitted exchanges, as in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The process model supplies an endpoint association on the two formal endpoint labels $-$ and $+$:\n\n$$\\rho_P:\\{-,+\\}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F,\\qquad \\rho_P(-)=\\mathbf x_P^-=(x_{1,P}^-,\\ldots,x_{m,P}^-),\\qquad \\rho_P(+)=\\mathbf x_P^+=(x_{1,P}^+,\\ldots,x_{m,P}^+),$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x_P^-$ and $\\mathbf x_P^+$ are respectively the declared constituent-state tuples representing the aggregate equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-$ and $x_P^+$ of $P$. Both tuples must belong to the same $\\mathcal F$. Restrict to endpoint splits admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] as a collection of independent separated macroscopic systems, so that its additive entropy rule applies. On this admitted $\\mathcal F$, define\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\mathbf x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i)\\qquad\\left(\\mathbf x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)\\in\\mathcal F\\right).$$\n\nThis uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]].\n\nThe adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(+))-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(-))\\ge0.$$\n\nThe process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(+))-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(-))=0$. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. The map $\\sigma_\\alpha$ takes values in numerical state coordinates; its physical SI state map is explicitly $\\chi_\\alpha=\\Phi_c\\circ\\sigma_\\alpha$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the numerical-to-SI unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Thus the physical amounts at this point are $n_j=m x_j$, and the numerical amount coordinates in $\\sigma_\\alpha$ are $n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Derivative-defined physical state functions are evaluated on $\\chi_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, or equivalently pulled back along $\\Phi_c$ to $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$ in this precise pullback sense, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-legendre-potentials",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$ with finite species list $(1,\\ldots,r)$ and a local energy representation $U_c=U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]]. Here $s$ is entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, $V>0$ is volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, each $n_j>0$ is in $\\mathrm{mol}$, $U_c$ is in joules, and the conjugates are $T=U_{c,s}$ in kelvins, $p=-U_{c,V}$ in pascals, and $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The derivatives hold every other listed natural coordinate fixed; the physical-coordinate and rest-frame conventions are those of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] and [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nFor an open chart, a **local Legendre transformation** means the following partial transformation of $U_c$: select one or more of its extensive coordinates $q$, take their conjugate coordinates $x=D_qU_c$, and use only a neighborhood on which $(q,z)\\mapsto(x,z)$, with $z$ the untransformed coordinates, has a $C^1$ inverse. Such a neighborhood is a valid local transform chart in the sense of [[def-td-response-functions]]. No inverse or potential as a function of the proposed natural coordinates is asserted outside that chart.\n\nOn their respective valid charts define the enthalpy, Helmholtz potential, Gibbs potential, and grand potential by\n\n$$H_c:=U_c+pV,\\qquad F_c:=U_c-Ts,\\qquad G_c:=U_c-Ts+pV,\\qquad \\Omega_c:=U_c-Ts-\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j.$$\n\nTheir natural-coordinate charts are respectively $(s,p,n)$, $(T,V,n)$, $(T,p,n)$, and $(T,V,\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r)$. In the volume transform the conjugate coordinate is $-p=U_{c,V}$, which accounts for the plus sign in $H_c$ and $G_c$; in the chemical transform the conjugates are $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$, which accounts for the minus signs in $\\Omega_c$. All four potentials have units of joules. A displayed differential or global convex-duality characterization is not part of this definition; critical, coexistence, and other non-C2 boundaries are excluded.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "pthm-td-potential-differentials",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On a valid local energy-representation chart from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]], let $s,V,n_j,U,T,p,\\mu_j$ and the potentials $H,F,G,\\Omega$ be as defined in [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. On every open neighborhood where the corresponding natural-coordinate maps have their stated $C^1$ inverses, the potentials are C2 and their differentials are\n\n$$dU=T\\,ds-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dH=T\\,ds+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$dF=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dG=-s\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$d\\Omega=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV-\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j.$$\n\nAll differentials are local state-function differentials. They do not assert that heat or work is an exact differential on process space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C$ be the open positive-temperature domain of a local C2 energy representation $\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ for a fixed material constitution $c$, with $V>0$, $n_j>0$, and conjugates $T=\\mathcal U_{c,s}$, $p=-\\mathcal U_{c,V}$, and $\\mu_j=\\mathcal U_{c,n_j}$. Let $W\\subseteq C$ be open. In addition to the framework suppliers, assume the **same-tag homogeneity hypothesis**: for every $q=(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in W$ there is an $\\varepsilon_q\\in(0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda n_r)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$$\n\nfor every $\\lambda>0$ with $|\\lambda-1|<\\varepsilon_q$ and $\\lambda q\\in C$. Then, throughout $W$,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c=Ts-pV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j,$$\n\nand on the same chart\n\n$$s\\,dT-V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j=0.$$\n\nThe same-tag hypothesis is supplied, in particular, when the inverse states $\\Psi(q):=(\\mathcal U_c(q),V,n)$ for $q\\in W$ lie in the designated common-law cone of [[post-td-common-bulk-constitutive-law]], expressed in physical SI coordinate values using the fixed unit conversion. On this branch the adopted entropy transport implies the energy homogeneity locally by uniqueness of the local inverse; no global inverse or global energy-domain cone is assumed. These are conditional model deductions. The general tagged relation in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] alone does not identify the scaled system with one fixed constitutive law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here \u201clocal graph dimension $k$\u201d means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], bilateral energy, volume, and species exchange give common $T_0,p_0$ and $\\mu_\\alpha=\\mu_\\beta$ at the attained interior coexistence point. Apply [F5] separately to the two phase branches using [A1]: $G_i/n_i=\\mu_i$ for $i=\\alpha,\\beta$. Thus $F(T_0,p_0):=g_\\beta(T_0,p_0)-g_\\alpha(T_0,p_0)=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "On each branch, hold the amount $n_i$ fixed while varying $(T,p)$. By [F2] and [F4], $d(G_i/n_i)=-s_i\\,dT+v_i\\,dp$. Consequently $F_T=-(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$ and $F_p=v_\\beta-v_\\alpha$ at $(T_0,p_0)$. The nonzero-volume-jump hypothesis makes $F_p\\ne0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [L1] to $F=g_\\beta-g_\\alpha$ on $O$. It gives a unique local C1 graph $p=p(T)$ satisfying $F(T,p(T))=0$. Differentiating this identity using [L2] gives $0=F_T+F_p\\,p'(T)$. Substitution of the derivatives from step 1.2 yields\n$$\np'(T_0)=\\frac{s_\\beta-s_\\alpha}{v_\\beta-v_\\alpha}.\n$$\nThe same calculation applies at every point of the shrunken coexistence interval with nonzero $\\Delta v$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the reversible latent-heat clause, the system entropy change when $n$ moles are converted is $n(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. By [A2], the full isolated compound has zero total entropy change; all other components have zero entropy change, and the reservoir contributes $-Q/T$, so $Q/T=n(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. Dividing by $n>0$ gives $\\ell_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}:=Q/n=T(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. This conclusion is conditional on the stated reversible operation and ideal reservoir; it is not a property of an arbitrary irreversible conversion path.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "empty: The empty boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\alpha,\\beta$ be two one-component phase branches from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] on a common open chart $O\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ with coordinates $(T,p)$. Assume each branch has a local $C^2$ energy representation $\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)$, $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, and that within each fixed branch tag it satisfies the same-tag homogeneity\n$$\n\\mathcal U_i(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)\n$$\nfor all $\\lambda$ in a neighborhood of $1$ for which the scaled state remains in the chart. This is a phase-branch hypothesis: the general tagged law of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] does not imply it. It may be stipulated independently on each local branch, or supplied by the common-law bulk subclass of [[pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem]] when each phase's local inverse states lie in its designated entropy cone with fixed species and intensive parameters and common SI units and energy/entropy reference conventions. That supplied subclass uses local inverse uniqueness and does not require a global energy inverse.\n\nSuppose a state $(T_0,p_0)\\in O$ is an attained interior coexistence equilibrium with bilateral energy, volume, and species exchange between the two branches, and both branches admit the valid local Gibbs charts of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Write $g_i=G_i/n_i$, $s_i=S_i/n_i$, and $v_i=V_i/n_i$ in the units specified by [[def-td-si-quantities]]. If\n$$\n\\Delta v(T_0,p_0):=v_\\beta(T_0,p_0)-v_\\alpha(T_0,p_0)\\ne0,\n$$\nthen, on some open interval about $T_0$, the equal-Gibbs-per-mole coexistence condition $g_\\alpha(T,p(T))=g_\\beta(T,p(T))$ defines a unique local $C^1$ curve and\n$$\n\\frac{dp}{dT}=\\frac{s_\\beta-s_\\alpha}{v_\\beta-v_\\alpha}.\n$$\nIf, in addition, conversion from $\\alpha$ to $\\beta$ is admitted as a reversible process in contact with an ideal thermal reservoir at the same fixed $T$, and the full finite isolated compound (converted phases, reservoir, work stores and all auxiliaries) has equilibrium endpoints with additive component entropy, with every component other than the converted phases and that reservoir having zero entropy change, then its molar latent heat absorbed by the system is $\\ell_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}=T(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. These are conditional model deductions. The slope conclusion is not asserted when $\\Delta v=0$ or where the branch charts are not $C^1$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f5dae9549440e9f1397d08596c95eaf7b04679110cf5289bdf6ec76b535a83f7",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
