      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-reversible-process",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-reversible-process.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $P=(I_P,x_P^-,x_P^+,z_P,\\kappa_P,r_P)$ be an admitted closed process record as defined in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$. The record is **reversible relative to its declared boundary and protocol** when the model also admits a reverse record $P^{\\mathrm{rev}}$ for the same system and material constitution, starting at $x_P^+$ and ending at $x_P^-$, such that every auxiliary and device state specified by the protocol is restored to its initial state and every signed boundary transfer is reversed. In particular,\n\n$$Q[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-Q[P],\\qquad W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P].$$\n\nIf the protocol records additional transfer channels, their signed values must likewise be negatives of their forward values. A record that is slow is not reversible merely because it is slow; it must satisfy the reverse-record condition above. Reversibility is therefore relative to what the model includes inside the boundary and which reverse controls it admits.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Ideal thermal reservoir and reversible conversion: the reservoir entropy change is $-Q/T$ when heat $Q$ is absorbed by the system, and an admitted reversible operation has zero total endpoint entropy change under [[def-td-reversible-process]] and [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]].",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
          "carrier": "items/post-td-macroscopic-second-law.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound, and restrict to constituent splits for which [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] supplies the additive total entropy. The adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^+)-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^-)\\ge0,$$\n\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is the sum of the constituent entropies in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. The process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt zero total entropy change. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Ideal thermal reservoir and reversible conversion: the reservoir entropy change is $-Q/T$ when heat $Q$ is absorbed by the system, and an admitted reversible operation has zero total endpoint entropy change under [[def-td-reversible-process]] and [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]].",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a constitution $c$ with $C\\ge1$ named species and its equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. An interior composition is represented by\n\n$$\nx=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{C-1})\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},\n$$\n\nwith $x_C:=1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$; for $C=1$, $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $x_1=1$.\n\nA **phase branch** $\\alpha$ is model data consisting of an open chart $W_\\alpha\\subseteq(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}\\times(0,\\infty)$ with coordinates $(T,p,x,m)$ and a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\\sigma_\\alpha:W_\\alpha\\to U_\\alpha$ onto an open subset $U_\\alpha\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{C+2}$ contained in the equilibrium state domain $D_c$. Here $T$ is in kelvins, $p$ is in pascals, $m$ is total amount in moles, and $C^1$ means continuous first coordinate derivatives as in [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The chart dimension is $C+2$, matching the ambient dimension of $D_c$. At $\\sigma_\\alpha(T,p,x,m)$, the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] give temperature $T$, pressure $p$, and mole fractions $x_j=n_j/m$; here $n_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=\\sum_{i=1}^C n_i=m$ uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]]. An **intensive phase branch** additionally has each derivative-defined chemical potential $\\mu_j(T,p,x,m)$ independent of $m$ throughout $W_\\alpha$; write its value as $\\mu_j^\\alpha(T,p,x)$. This scale invariance is an explicit constitutive model property, not a consequence of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A valid local response chart and its inverse are as in [[def-td-response-functions]] and [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Existence of $W_\\alpha$ or $\\sigma_\\alpha$ is a model premise, not a consequence of the equations of thermodynamics.\n\nOn a branch with a valid natural-variable chart, $G^\\alpha$ denotes the Gibbs potential of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]] restricted to the branch. For a one-component branch with amount $m_\\alpha>0$, define $g_\\alpha:=G^\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, $s_\\alpha:=S_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, and $v_\\alpha:=V_\\alpha/m_\\alpha$, in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, respectively. These quotients need not be independent of amount, and $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ is not assumed by this definition. Such an equality requires a separate same-tag homogeneity hypothesis on the branch.\n\nA **phase mixture** of a finite list of branches is a feasible separated compound whose positive phase amounts $m_\\alpha$ have fractions $\\lambda_\\alpha:=m_\\alpha/\\sum_\\beta m_\\beta>0$ with $\\sum_\\alpha\\lambda_\\alpha=1$. Its extensive coordinates and entropy are those supplied by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] and [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The mixture is in **coexistence** when it is an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint under the declared totals and permitted exchanges, as in [[post-td-entropy-maximum]]. This definition does not assert that a maximum exists, that all branches can exchange every coordinate, or that metastable states are global equilibria.\n\nThe bulk scaling postulate may send a material tag $c$ to a different tag $c^{(\\lambda)}$. It therefore does not supply same-tag degree-one homogeneity of $G^\\alpha$ or $U^\\alpha$ on a phase branch. Any result using $g_\\alpha=\\mu^\\alpha$ must state that additional hypothesis.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]]: a one-component branch supplies $g=G/n$, $s=S/n$, and $v=V/n$ on its valid positive-temperature chart; the definition does not assert $g=\\mu$ without same-tag homogeneity.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-td-legendre-potentials",
          "carrier": "items/def-td-legendre-potentials.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ with finite species list $(1,\\ldots,r)$ and a local energy representation $U_c=U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]]. Here $s$ is entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, $V>0$ is volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, each $n_j>0$ is in $\\mathrm{mol}$, $U_c$ is in joules, and the conjugates are $T=U_{c,s}$ in kelvins, $p=-U_{c,V}$ in pascals, and $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The derivatives hold every other listed natural coordinate fixed; the physical-coordinate and rest-frame conventions are those of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] and [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nFor an open chart, a **local Legendre transformation** means the following partial transformation of $U_c$: select one or more of its extensive coordinates $q$, take their conjugate coordinates $x=D_qU_c$, and use only a neighborhood on which $(q,z)\\mapsto(x,z)$, with $z$ the untransformed coordinates, has a $C^1$ inverse. Such a neighborhood is a valid local transform chart in the sense of [[def-td-response-functions]]. No inverse or potential as a function of the proposed natural coordinates is asserted outside that chart.\n\nOn their respective valid charts define the enthalpy, Helmholtz potential, Gibbs potential, and grand potential by\n\n$$H_c:=U_c+pV,\\qquad F_c:=U_c-Ts,\\qquad G_c:=U_c-Ts+pV,\\qquad \\Omega_c:=U_c-Ts-\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j.$$\n\nTheir natural-coordinate charts are respectively $(s,p,n)$, $(T,V,n)$, $(T,p,n)$, and $(T,V,\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r)$. In the volume transform the conjugate coordinate is $-p=U_{c,V}$, which accounts for the plus sign in $H_c$ and $G_c$; in the chemical transform the conjugates are $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$, which accounts for the minus signs in $\\Omega_c$. All four potentials have units of joules. A displayed differential or global convex-duality characterization is not part of this definition; critical, coexistence, and other non-C2 boundaries are excluded.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-td-legendre-potentials]]: on its valid local natural-variable chart, $G=U-Ts+pV$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $P\\ge1$ named phase branches of the same finite species list occupy a separated compound at an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint with the fixed totals and exact feasible set declared by [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. Assume each phase lies in an open C2 single-phase chart with finite positive temperature. For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.\n\nThen, for every pair $\\alpha,\\beta$:\n- if energy exchange is permitted, $T_\\alpha=T_\\beta$;\n- if volume exchange is permitted, $p_\\alpha/T_\\alpha=p_\\beta/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $p_\\alpha=p_\\beta$;\n- if species $j$ is transferable, $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}/T_\\alpha=\\mu_{\\beta,j}/T_\\beta$, and if energy exchange is also permitted then $\\mu_{\\alpha,j}=\\mu_{\\beta,j}$.\n\nThe pressure and chemical potentials are the derivative-defined state functions of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The theorem makes no equality claim for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or a channel absent from the declared feasible set. It is a model deduction, not evidence that these phases or exchange channels occur in nature.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions]]: with energy, volume, and species transfer feasible in both directions at an attained interior entropy maximum, the two branches have equal positive temperatures, equal pressures, and equal chemical potentials.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-potential-differentials",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-potential-differentials.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "On a valid local energy-representation chart from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]], let $s,V,n_j,U,T,p,\\mu_j$ and the potentials $H,F,G,\\Omega$ be as defined in [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. On every open neighborhood where the corresponding natural-coordinate maps have their stated $C^1$ inverses, the potentials are C2 and their differentials are\n\n$$dU=T\\,ds-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dH=T\\,ds+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$dF=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dG=-s\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$d\\Omega=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV-\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j.$$\n\nAll differentials are local state-function differentials. They do not assert that heat or work is an exact differential on process space.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-potential-differentials]]: on a valid local C2 energy-representation chart, $dG=-S\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\mu\\,dn$. Its current batch-9 carrier is registered with draft status and its proof is conditional on the actual local-energy and partial-Legendre suppliers; their interfaces and proof statuses are recorded in batch-13 cross-batch evidence.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-9 draft; the differential statement matches the use, conditional on its actual local-energy and partial-Legendre suppliers.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $C$ be the open positive-temperature domain of a local C2 energy representation $\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ for a fixed material constitution $c$, with $V>0$, $n_j>0$, and conjugates $T=\\mathcal U_{c,s}$, $p=-\\mathcal U_{c,V}$, and $\\mu_j=\\mathcal U_{c,n_j}$. Let $W\\subseteq C$ be open. In addition to the framework suppliers, assume the **same-tag homogeneity hypothesis**: for every $q=(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in W$ there is an $\\varepsilon_q\\in(0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n_1,\\ldots,\\lambda n_r)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$$\n\nfor every $\\lambda>0$ with $|\\lambda-1|<\\varepsilon_q$ and $\\lambda q\\in C$. Then, throughout $W$,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_c=Ts-pV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j,$$\n\nand on the same chart\n\n$$s\\,dT-V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j=0.$$\n\nThe same-tag hypothesis is supplied, in particular, when the inverse states $\\Psi(q):=(\\mathcal U_c(q),V,n)$ for $q\\in W$ lie in the designated common-law cone of [[post-td-common-bulk-constitutive-law]], expressed in physical SI coordinate values using the fixed unit conversion. On this branch the adopted entropy transport implies the energy homogeneity locally by uniqueness of the local inverse; no global inverse or global energy-domain cone is assumed. These are conditional model deductions. The general tagged relation in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] alone does not identify the scaled system with one fixed constitutive law.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem]]: if the same-tag homogeneity [A1] holds on a valid local C2 energy chart, its current Statement gives $U=Ts-pV+\\mu n$, hence $G/n=\\mu$. Its general conditional implication requires [A1], and its separately designated common-law bulk subclass derives that local premise from adopted entropy transport and local inverse uniqueness. Neither route silently equates distinct physical system tags.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current owner-repaired draft; exact general local homogeneity implication retained, with separately adopted common-law bulk premise discharged through local inverse uniqueness. Focused same-owner interface review completed; no independent native proof acceptance claimed.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here \u201clocal graph dimension $k$\u201d means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank]]: for a C1 scalar equation $F(T,p)=0$ on an open subset of $\\mathbb R^2$, if $F(T_0,p_0)=0$ and $\\partial_pF(T_0,p_0)\\ne0$, the local zero set is uniquely a C1 graph $p=p(T)$ with $p'=-F_T/F_p$. The current carrier is registered in batch 4; it remains a draft mathematical item, not an independent acceptance.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-4 draft; its local-graph interface matches the stated nonsingular-minor hypotheses. Proof remains draft, not independent acceptance.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "carrier": "items/thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives.md",
          "status": "current published supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]: differentiating a composition of differentiable maps gives the composed derivative.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-generated; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 2.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "/root/resolve_td8_tagged_homogeneity focused consumer review, ongoing round2",
        "notes": "Read exact final drained native13 source and current repaired Euler supplier. General phase-local homogeneity remains sufficient, the designated bulk subclass can supply it under inverse-chart/reference hypotheses, and smooth Gibbs charts, bilateral exchange, positive amount, nonzero jump and reversible reservoir conditions are retained. Four consumer closure reviewed; unchanged proofs/derivation step IDs retained."
      }
    },
    "ex-td-clapeyron-linear-branches": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-clapeyron-local",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\alpha,\\beta$ be two one-component phase branches from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] on a common open chart $O\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ with coordinates $(T,p)$. Assume each branch has a local $C^2$ energy representation $\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)$, $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, and that within each fixed branch tag it satisfies the same-tag homogeneity\n$$\n\\mathcal U_i(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)\n$$\nfor all $\\lambda$ in a neighborhood of $1$ for which the scaled state remains in the chart. This is a phase-branch hypothesis: the general tagged law of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] does not imply it. It may be stipulated independently on each local branch, or supplied by the common-law bulk subclass of [[pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem]] when each phase's local inverse states lie in its designated entropy cone with fixed species and intensive parameters and common SI units and energy/entropy reference conventions. That supplied subclass uses local inverse uniqueness and does not require a global energy inverse.\n\nSuppose a state $(T_0,p_0)\\in O$ is an attained interior coexistence equilibrium with bilateral energy, volume, and species exchange between the two branches, and both branches admit the valid local Gibbs charts of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Write $g_i=G_i/n_i$, $s_i=S_i/n_i$, and $v_i=V_i/n_i$ in the units specified by [[def-td-si-quantities]]. If\n$$\n\\Delta v(T_0,p_0):=v_\\beta(T_0,p_0)-v_\\alpha(T_0,p_0)\\ne0,\n$$\nthen, on some open interval about $T_0$, the equal-Gibbs-per-mole coexistence condition $g_\\alpha(T,p(T))=g_\\beta(T,p(T))$ defines a unique local $C^1$ curve and\n$$\n\\frac{dp}{dT}=\\frac{s_\\beta-s_\\alpha}{v_\\beta-v_\\alpha}.\n$$\nIf, in addition, conversion from $\\alpha$ to $\\beta$ is admitted as a reversible process in contact with an ideal thermal reservoir at the same fixed $T$, and the full finite isolated compound (converted phases, reservoir, work stores and all auxiliaries) has equilibrium endpoints with additive component entropy, with every component other than the converted phases and that reservoir having zero entropy change, then its molar latent heat absorbed by the system is $\\ell_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}=T(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. These are conditional model deductions. The slope conclusion is not asserted when $\\Delta v=0$ or where the branch charts are not $C^1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "At $(T_0,p_0)$, differentiation gives $S_i/n_i=s_i$, $V_i/n_i=v_i$, and $\\mu_i=g_i=g_0$. Also $U_{i,T}=n_i c$, $V_{i,p}=-n_i\\kappa$, and $V_{i,T}=0$ there, so the derivative determinant for $(T,p)\\mapsto(U_i,V_i)$ at fixed positive amount is $-n_i^2c\\kappa\\ne0$. The functions are smooth, so [L1] gives valid local Gibbs state charts. Hence $\\Delta s=3\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$ and $\\Delta v=3.0\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}\\ne0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] under the explicitly stipulated model assumptions [A1]:\n$$\n\\left.\\frac{dp}{dT}\\right|_{(T_0,p_0)}=\\frac{3}{3.0\\times10^{-3}}=1.0\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,K^{-1}},\\qquad \\ell_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}=T_0\\Delta s=300(3)=900\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}.\n$$\nThese are exact values for the constructed model only.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "empty: The empty boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $T_0=300\\,\\mathrm K$, $p_0=10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$, $g_0=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $c>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\kappa>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$. On a common open chart define\n$$\ng_i(T,p)=g_0-s_i(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_i(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2,\n$$\nfor $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, with $s_\\alpha=2\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $s_\\beta=5\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $v_\\alpha=1.0\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, and $v_\\beta=4.0\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$. Set $G_i=n_i g_i$ and use the natural Gibbs assignments $S_i=-\\partial_TG_i$, $V_i=\\partial_pG_i$, $\\mu_i=\\partial_{n_i}G_i$, and $U_i=G_i+TS_i-pV_i$. Thus same-tag homogeneity is explicit. Assume the constructed model stipulates an attained coexistence endpoint with bilateral energy, volume, and species exchange, and a reversible $\\alpha\\to\\beta$ conversion in contact with an ideal thermal reservoir at $T_0$. The reversible conversion is a record of the complete finite isolated compound comprising converted phases, the fixed-temperature reservoir, work stores and all auxiliaries, with equilibrium endpoints and additive component entropy. Every component other than the converted phases and reservoir has zero entropy change, and the reservoir change is $-Q/T_0$. These are the exact additional entropy premises of the local Clapeyron theorem. These parameters and endpoint assumptions are model inputs, not empirical claims.",
