      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-phase-dimension.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-tdmath-phase-dimension]]: For $C=1$, $P=2$, $R=0$, a coexistence-equation zero with a nonsingular derivative minor has local equation-set dimension $C-P+2=1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current registered batch-4 draft; its C1 equation-zero-set dimension interface matches. Proof remains draft, not independent acceptance.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the entropy of each phase is $C^2$ on its physical state chart and its derivative-defined temperature at the endpoint is finite and positive. The equation-set dimension below concerns this smooth interior state only.\n\nLet $C\\ge1$ be the number of named species, $P\\ge1$ the number of coexisting phase branches, and $R\\ge0$ the number of independent internal reaction directions. Consider an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For each phase $\\alpha$, use an intensive phase branch from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and an interior composition $x^\\alpha\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}$. Let each branch chemical-potential map\n\\[\n\\mu^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)=(\\mu_1^\\alpha,\\ldots,\\mu_C^\\alpha)\n\\]\nbe $C^1$ on a common open domain\n\\[\n\\mathcal U=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times(\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ})^P\n\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\,n},\\qquad n=2+P(C-1).\n\\]\nAssume energy, volume, and every named species can be exchanged between every phase pair in both directions, and every listed reaction direction is feasible in both extent directions in every phase while that phase's energy, volume, other reaction extents, and all other phase coordinates are held fixed at the coexistence point. Let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent, dimensionless stoichiometric vectors, with extents measured in moles. Phase equilibrium imposes $\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\mu_i^1=0$ for $1\\le i\\le C$, $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$; internal reaction equilibrium imposes $(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$ in every phase for $1\\le r\\le R$. On the phase-equality locus these are the same $R$ equations in each phase, so count them once using phase 1. Put $q:=C(P-1)$ and $m:=q+R$. If $m=0$ (equivalently $P=1,R=0$), no equation is imposed and the local zero set is the whole chart, of dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2-R$. If $m\\ge1$ and the combined equation map has an invertible $m\\times m$ derivative minor at the solution, with $m\\le n$, then the local zero set of these first-order coexistence-and-affinity equations is a graph of dimension\n\\[\nn-q-R=C-P+2-R.\n\\]\nThis is the dimension of the equation zero set in intensive coordinates only; phase amounts and their extensive conservation constraints are not counted, and the actual stable-coexistence set may be a proper subset after additional stability constraints. If the nonsingular-minor condition fails, no dimension formula is asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule]]: In the thermodynamic application, the same rank formula is conditional on an attained stable endpoint and the stated exchange hypotheses; this example verifies only the equation-map part.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem.md",
          "status": "current published supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]]: A $C^1$ map between open Euclidean sets with invertible derivative at a point has a local $C^1$ inverse there.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "batch-13-author self-audit",
        "notes": "Current numbered steps, exact linked interfaces, units, physical assumptions, and applicable boundaries/finite calculations were checked by the author. This is not independent approval."
      }
    },
    "ex-td-reactive-phase-rule-three-component": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$, with $r$ named species and dimensionless equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in D_c$, define the diagonal SI coordinate map\n\n$$\\Phi_c(x):=(U_c(x),V_c(x),n_{c,1}(x),\\ldots,n_{c,r}(x))=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n_1\\,\\mathrm{mol},\\ldots,\\hat n_r\\,\\mathrm{mol}).$$\n\nIn SI numerical coordinates this is a diagonal linear bijection with a diagonal linear inverse, so it maps ambient open sets to open sets and its inverse is defined on its image.\n\nChoose an open subset $O\\subseteq D_c$ in the ambient dimensionless Euclidean coordinates that is designated as a single-phase chart, and set $\\Omega:=\\Phi_c(O)$, an open chart of physical SI coordinate values. Here $U$ is the internal-energy coordinate evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. If $S_c:D_c\\to\\mathbb R$ is the entropy adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], define its physical-coordinate representative on $\\Omega$ by\n\n$$S_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R.$$\n\nWhen its arguments are the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, write this representative as $S_c(U,V,n)$ for brevity. On $\\Omega$, assume the finite representative is $C^2$ and has $S_{c,U}>0$, as adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in the physical $U$ coordinate while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; in particular the conversion factor from $\\hat U$ is included.\n\nExplicitly, with the partial derivatives on the right taken in the dimensionless coordinates on $O$ while holding every other hatted coordinate fixed, the physical partials are\n\n$$S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat U}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat V}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat n_j}S_c,$$\n\nusing the coordinate-conversion convention of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]].\n\nDefine the physical-coordinate representatives of the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}},\\qquad p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=-T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}.$$\n\nFor a state-domain point $x\\in O$, define the corresponding state functions by pullback, $T_c(x):=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, $p_c(x):=p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(x):=\\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$. When the argument is a physical tuple $(U,V,n)$, write $T_c(U,V,n)$, $p_c(U,V,n)$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(U,V,n)$ for the physical-coordinate representatives. Thus both notations refer to the same state functions through $\\Phi_c$.\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the entropy of each phase is $C^2$ on its physical state chart and its derivative-defined temperature at the endpoint is finite and positive. The equation-set dimension below concerns this smooth interior state only.\n\nLet $C\\ge1$ be the number of named species, $P\\ge1$ the number of coexisting phase branches, and $R\\ge0$ the number of independent internal reaction directions. Consider an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For each phase $\\alpha$, use an intensive phase branch from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and an interior composition $x^\\alpha\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}$. Let each branch chemical-potential map\n$$\n\\mu^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)=(\\mu_1^\\alpha,\\ldots,\\mu_C^\\alpha)\n$$\nbe $C^1$ on a common open domain\n$$\n\\mathcal U=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times(\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ})^P \\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\,n},\\qquad n=2+P(C-1).\n$$\nAssume energy, volume, and every named species can be exchanged between every phase pair in both directions, and every listed reaction direction is feasible in both extent directions in every phase while that phase's energy, volume, other reaction extents, and all other phase coordinates are held fixed at the coexistence point. Let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent, dimensionless stoichiometric vectors, with extents measured in moles. Phase equilibrium imposes $\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\mu_i^1=0$ for $1\\le i\\le C$, $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$; internal reaction equilibrium imposes $(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$ in every phase for $1\\le r\\le R$. On the phase-equality locus these are the same $R$ equations in each phase, so count them once using phase 1. Put $q:=C(P-1)$ and $m:=q+R$. If $m=0$ (equivalently $P=1,R=0$), no equation is imposed and the local zero set is the whole chart, of dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2-R$. If $m\\ge1$ and the combined equation map has an invertible $m\\times m$ derivative minor at the solution, with $m\\le n$, then the local zero set of these first-order coexistence-and-affinity equations is a graph of dimension\n$$\nn-q-R=C-P+2-R.\n$$\nThis is the dimension of the equation zero set in intensive coordinates only; phase amounts and their extensive conservation constraints are not counted, and the actual stable-coexistence set may be a proper subset after additional stability constraints. If the nonsingular-minor condition fails, no dimension formula is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating the conjugate assignments in [A1] gives $dG=-S\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_j\\mu_j\\,dn_j$; differentiating $U=G+TS-pV$ gives $dU=T\\,dS-p\\,dV+\\sum_j\\mu_j\\,dn_j$. Thus $S_U=1/T$, $S_V=p/T$, and $S_{n_j}=-\\mu_j/T$, as in [F2]. From the displayed $g^\\gamma$, at the center $S_T=m_\\gamma c/T_0$, $U_T=m_\\gamma c$, $V_T=0$, and $V_p=-m_\\gamma\\kappa$, so the fixed-composition/amount $(T,p)$ Jacobian for $(U,V)$ is $-m_\\gamma^2c\\kappa$. The map $(x_1,x_2,m_\\gamma)\\mapsto(n_1,n_2,n_3)$ has determinant $m_\\gamma^2(x_1+x_2+x_3)=m_\\gamma^2$. Hence the full state-map Jacobian is $-m_\\gamma^4c\\kappa\\ne0$, and [L2] gives a local physical state chart.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "L2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $m=n_1+n_2+n_3$ and $x_j=n_j/m$, [L1] at fixed $T,p$ and the other two species amounts gives\n$$\n\\mu_1=g+(1-x_1)g_{x_1}-x_2g_{x_2},\\quad \\mu_2=g-x_1g_{x_1}+(1-x_2)g_{x_2},\\quad \\mu_3=g-x_1g_{x_1}-x_2g_{x_2}.\n$$\nSubstitution of the two stated $g^\\gamma$ at the centered point gives $\\mu^\\alpha=\\mu^\\beta=(0,0,0)$. Thus $F_i=\\mu_i^\\beta-\\mu_i^\\alpha=0$ and $A=\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "In columns $(x_1^\\beta,x_2^\\beta,p,x_1^\\alpha)$, the derivative minor of $(F_1,F_2,F_3,A)$ at the centered point is\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\eta/3&-\\eta/3&0&0\\\\ -\\eta/3&2\\eta/3&0&0\\\\ -\\eta/3&-\\eta/3&q&0\\\\ 0&0&0&-5a_\\alpha/6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\nIt is nonsingular: the upper-left $2\\times2$ block has determinant $\\eta^2/3\\ne0$, and the remaining diagonal factors are $q>0$ and $-5a_\\alpha/6\\ne0$. The combined equation map therefore has the required rank four.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "The ambient coordinate dimension is $n=2+P(C-1)=6$, and the number of equations is $q+R=C(P-1)+R=3+1=4$. By [F1], the local equation zero set has dimension $6-4=2$, agreeing with $C-P+2-R=3-2+2-1=2$. This is only the full-rank equation-set calculation.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "empty: The empty boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Take $C=3$ species and $P=2$ phases. Write $x_3:=1-x_1-x_2$, and center the open composition charts at $x_1=x_2=x_3=1/3$. Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $s_0,c,v_0,\\kappa,q>0$, and $\\eta=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The parameters have units $s_0,c\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $v_0\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$, and $q\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$. For $\\gamma\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, set $a_\\alpha=2\\eta$, $a_\\beta=\\eta$, $q_\\alpha=0$, and $q_\\beta=q$, and define molar Gibbs potentials\n$$\ng^\\gamma(T,p,x)=-s_0(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_0(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{2}(x_1^2+x_2^2)-\\frac{a_\\gamma}{3}(x_1+x_2)+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{9}+q_\\gamma(p-p_0)x_3.\n$$\nSet $G^\\gamma=m_\\gamma g^\\gamma$ on each fixed-tag branch and adopt the natural Gibbs assignments $S=-G_T$, $V=G_p$, and $\\mu_j=G_{n_j}$, with $U=G+TS-pV$. These smooth charts are scale-invariant in amount. Near the centered point the volumes are positive, and the $(U,V)$ chart Jacobian at fixed composition and amount is nonsingular: $U_T=m_\\gamma c$, $V_p=-m_\\gamma\\kappa$, and $V_T=0$ there, so its determinant is $-m_\\gamma^2c\\kappa\\ne0$. The reaction vector is $\\nu=(-1,-1/2,1)^{\\mathsf T}$, of rank $R=1$. At $(T_0,p_0,x_1,x_2)=(T_0,p_0,1/3,1/3)$, both phases have $\\mu^\\gamma=(0,0,0)$, so the phase-equality equations and reaction affinity vanish.\n\nThe constructed branch's local physical state map is $\\chi:(T,p,n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$ (using the positive amount and interior composition coordinates displayed here). On the inverse patch verified below, define its numerical domain $O_c=\\Phi_c^{-1}(\\chi(\\mathcal O))$ and declare this patch to be the local model's $D_c$ patch, where $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$ is the unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Define the numerical entropy by $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$, with $S^{\\rm SI}$ supplied by the Gibbs assignments through this inverse. Thus the displayed derivatives are derivatives in physical SI coordinates on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$; the model patch is not an empirical claim about an independently specified material domain.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "6e4fb88082d024e867949a6cc87c6fd00ff6233e519e0b8ad219ce633b18443c",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
