    "ex-td-reaction-boundary-example": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-reaction-boundary-equilibrium",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $C\\ge1$ species and $R\\ge1$ declared internal reaction extents. Let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{C\\times R}$ be the dimensionless stoichiometric matrix, let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^C$ have units mol, and let $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R$ have units mol. The amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$, and the nonnegative-amount feasible extent set is\n$$\n\\mathcal F_\\nu:=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^R:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^C\\}.\n$$\nFix $T_0>0$ and $p_0$ in a declared natural-variable chart. Let $\\Phi(\\xi)$ be the constrained Gibbs potential on an open neighborhood $U$ of an equilibrium extent $\\xi_*$, and assume the model supplies a finite $C^1$ extension through every zero-amount boundary point under consideration. On $U$, assume\n$$\nD\\Phi(\\xi)[h]=\\sum_{j=1}^C\\mu_j(\\xi)(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi))\\cdot h,\n$$\nwhere $\\mu_j$ are the molar chemical potentials in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. This extension and derivative identity are not supplied by the positive-mole phase chart itself.\n\nSuppose $\\xi_*$ is an attained stable equilibrium for the fixed-$T_0,p_0$ internal reaction model with the ideal reservoirs of [[pthm-td-fixed-tp-reaction-potential-minimum]]. Then $\\xi_*$ minimizes $\\Phi$ on the exact reaction-feasible set locally. Let $J_*:=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. The feasible tangent cone is\n$$\nT_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*):=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^R:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J_*\\}.\n$$\nThe first-order necessary condition is\n$$\n(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*))\\cdot h\\ge0\\qquad(h\\in T_{\\mathcal F_\\nu}(\\xi_*)).\n$$\nIf $\\xi_*$ is interior to $\\mathcal F_\\nu$, this reduces to $\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu(\\xi_*)=0$. If, in addition, $\\Phi$ is convex and differentiable on a convex neighborhood containing the relevant feasible set, the same tangent inequality is sufficient for global minimization there.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Here species $A$ is active because $n_A(\\xi_*)=0$. A tangent extent $h$ must satisfy $(\\nu h)_A=-h\\ge0$, so $h\\le0$. The affinity vector is the scalar $\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu=-\\mu_A+\\mu_B=-1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. Hence $(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)h\\ge0$ for every feasible tangent extent $h\\le0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Directly, $\\Phi(\\xi)-\\Phi(0)=-(1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}})\\xi\\ge0$ for every feasible $\\xi\\in[-1,0]$ mol, so the boundary point is a global minimum on this interval. It need not satisfy the interior equality $\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
