    "ex-td-finite-two-state-partition-smooth": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carath\u00e9odory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The finite sum gives $Z=1+e^{-\\beta\\epsilon}>1$, so $F$ is finite for every $\\beta>0$. Differentiating with [F2] yields\n$$\nF'(\\beta)=\\frac{\\log Z(\\beta)}{\\beta^2}+\\frac{\\epsilon e^{-\\beta\\epsilon}}{\\beta Z(\\beta)},\n$$\nwhose terms are continuous on $\\beta>0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Each derivative of $e^{-\\beta\\epsilon}$ is $(-\\epsilon)^m e^{-\\beta\\epsilon}$ by induction on $m$, so $Z$ is C1 of every finite order. Since $Z>0$, repeated application of the logarithm derivative formula and quotient/product rules makes every derivative of $\\log Z$ continuous on $\\beta>0$. The same induction gives smoothness of $\\beta^{-1}$ there. Thus $F=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z$ is C1 of every finite order on $\\beta>0$. The finite model has no nonanalytic free-energy point at positive finite temperature; this says nothing about a sequence limit.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
