    "cex-td-noncompact-phase-mixture-no-attained-maximum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty compact set of dimensionless generator coordinates. Each $x\\in K$ labels one model-specified phase-generator state; the model assigns it entropy per mole $s(x)\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $s:K\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous, using the numerical entropy value in that fixed unit. Define $\\operatorname{conv}K$ to be the set of all finite convex combinations of points of $K$, and fix $\\bar x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$. Use the Borel sigma algebra relative to $K$. A Borel probability mixture is a countably additive probability measure $P$ on that sigma algebra; its barycenter and entropy per mole are the coordinatewise integrals $\\int_Kx\\,dP$ and $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. For $x\\in K$, define the unit point mass $\\delta_x$ by $\\delta_x(A)=1$ if $x\\in A$ and $\\delta_x(A)=0$ otherwise, for Borel $A\\subseteq K$. Assume every Borel probability mixture with barycenter $\\bar x$ is feasible and has entropy per mole $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. Then there are $m\\le d+1$, points $x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in K$, and weights $\\theta_i\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i=1$ such that $P_*:=\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i\\delta_{x_i}$ is a feasible Borel probability mixture and $\\int_Ks\\,dP_*\\ge\\int_Ks\\,dP$ for every feasible mixture $P$.\n\nHere integrals use the measure convention of [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. The entropy map is part of the model; this theorem derives neither its microscopic meaning nor compactness of a physical phase domain.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $0< s_0-a\\le s(x)\\le s_0$, every feasible $s$ is nonnegative and bounded. For $n\\ge2$, put $A_n:=\\{x\\in K:|x|\\ge1/n\\}$. Their union is $K$; since $P(K)=1$, countable additivity implies $P(A_n)>0$ for some $n$. On $A_n$, $s\\le s_0-a/n$, and on its complement $s\\le s_0$. For any nonnegative simple function $\\varphi\\le s$, the measure integral definition [F2] therefore gives\n$$\n\\int_K\\varphi\\,dP\\le (s_0-a/n)P(A_n)+s_0P(K\\setminus A_n)=s_0-aP(A_n)/n<s_0.\n$$\nIndeed, refine the finitely many level sets of $\\varphi$ by $A_n$ and its complement; each simple-function coefficient is bounded by the corresponding pointwise bound on that piece, so the simple-integral formula [F2] gives the inequality. The common strict upper bound is independent of $\\varphi$, hence its supremum also satisfies $\\int_Ks\\,dP<s_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each $0<\\epsilon<1$, the measure $P_\\epsilon=\\tfrac12\\delta_{-\\epsilon}+\\tfrac12\\delta_{\\epsilon}$ is supported on $K$, has mean zero, and satisfies $\\int s\\,dP_\\epsilon=s_0-a\\epsilon$. Letting $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ shows that the supremum is $s_0$. Step 1.1 shows that no feasible measure attains it. This lies outside the compactness hypothesis in [F1] and exhibits a finite supremum without a maximizing mixture.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
