      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture",
          "carrier": "items/pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, and let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty compact set of dimensionless phase-generator coordinates. A generator point $x\\in K$ encodes one model-specified homogeneous phase state, with declared entropy per mole $s(x)$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$; let $s:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Define $\\operatorname{conv}K$ as the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$, and fix $\\bar x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$. Use the Borel sigma algebra relative to $K$, and let $\\delta_x$ denote unit point mass at $x$. The vector integral $\\int_Kx\\,dP$ is taken coordinatewise. Assume every Borel probability mixture $P$ on $K$ with $\\int_Kx\\,dP(x)=\\bar x$ is feasible and has entropy per mole $\\int_Ks(x)\\,dP(x)$. Then the entropy maximum over all such mixtures is attained by a finite atomic mixture with at most $d+1$ phase-generator states.\n\nHere the integral uses the measure convention of [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. The entropy map includes its physical entropy units and is part of the model; this theorem does not derive the microscopic meaning of entropy or assert that a physical phase domain is compact.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture]]: Under these compactness, continuity, barycenter, and feasibility hypotheses, an entropy-maximizing mixture exists with at most $d+1$ atoms, here $d=1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current proof provenance is ai-altered; no independent acceptance is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-borel-sigma-algebra.md",
          "status": "current published supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]], [[def-probability-measure]], and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]: A Borel probability measure on finite $K$ assigns nonnegative weights summing to one, and the integral of a simple function is the finite weighted sum of its values.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-probability-measure",
          "carrier": "items/def-probability-measure.md",
          "status": "current published supplier; exact interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]], [[def-probability-measure]], and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]: A Borel probability measure on finite $K$ assigns nonnegative weights summing to one, and the integral of a simple function is the finite weighted sum of its values.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-measure-integral.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; exact interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where the Borel sets of the extended real line are the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]], [[def-probability-measure]], and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]: A Borel probability measure on finite $K$ assigns nonnegative weights summing to one, and the integral of a simple function is the finite weighted sum of its values.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Definition/Postulate interface inspected; no proof obligation.",
          "consumer_use": "Used in 1.1, 2.1."
        }
      ],
