    "cex-td-clapeyron-zero-volume-jump": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-clapeyron-local",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\alpha,\\beta$ be two one-component phase branches from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] on a common open chart $O\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ with coordinates $(T,p)$. Assume each branch has a local $C^2$ energy representation $\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)$, $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, and that within each fixed branch tag it satisfies the same-tag homogeneity\n$$\n\\mathcal U_i(\\lambda s,\\lambda V,\\lambda n)=\\lambda\\mathcal U_i(s,V,n)\n$$\nfor all $\\lambda$ in a neighborhood of $1$ for which the scaled state remains in the chart. This is a phase-branch hypothesis: the general tagged law of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] does not imply it. It may be stipulated independently on each local branch, or supplied by the common-law bulk subclass of [[pthm-td-euler-and-gibbs-duhem]] when each phase's local inverse states lie in its designated entropy cone with fixed species and intensive parameters and common SI units and energy/entropy reference conventions. That supplied subclass uses local inverse uniqueness and does not require a global energy inverse.\n\nSuppose a state $(T_0,p_0)\\in O$ is an attained interior coexistence equilibrium with bilateral energy, volume, and species exchange between the two branches, and both branches admit the valid local Gibbs charts of [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. Write $g_i=G_i/n_i$, $s_i=S_i/n_i$, and $v_i=V_i/n_i$ in the units specified by [[def-td-si-quantities]]. If\n$$\n\\Delta v(T_0,p_0):=v_\\beta(T_0,p_0)-v_\\alpha(T_0,p_0)\\ne0,\n$$\nthen, on some open interval about $T_0$, the equal-Gibbs-per-mole coexistence condition $g_\\alpha(T,p(T))=g_\\beta(T,p(T))$ defines a unique local $C^1$ curve and\n$$\n\\frac{dp}{dT}=\\frac{s_\\beta-s_\\alpha}{v_\\beta-v_\\alpha}.\n$$\nIf, in addition, conversion from $\\alpha$ to $\\beta$ is admitted as a reversible process in contact with an ideal thermal reservoir at the same fixed $T$, and the full finite isolated compound (converted phases, reservoir, work stores and all auxiliaries) has equilibrium endpoints with additive component entropy, with every component other than the converted phases and that reservoir having zero entropy change, then its molar latent heat absorbed by the system is $\\ell_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}=T(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)$. These are conditional model deductions. The slope conclusion is not asserted when $\\Delta v=0$ or where the branch charts are not $C^1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "At $(T_0,p_0)$, differentiation gives $S_i/n_i=s_i$, $V_i/n_i=v_0$, and $\\mu_i=g_i=g_0$. Also $U_{i,T}=n_i c$, $V_{i,p}=-n_i\\kappa$, and $V_{i,T}=0$, so the derivative determinant for $(T,p)\\mapsto(U_i,V_i)$ at fixed positive amount is $-n_i^2c\\kappa\\ne0$. Thus [L1] supplies valid local state charts. The branch equality function is\n$$\nF(T,p):=g_\\beta-g_\\alpha=-(s_\\beta-s_\\alpha)(T-T_0),\n$$\nwhose zero set is $T=T_0$ with every nearby chart pressure permitted.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Here $\\Delta v=v_\\beta-v_\\alpha=0$, so the zero set is not the graph of a unique pressure as a function of temperature on an open interval about $T_0$, and the quotient $\\Delta s/\\Delta v$ is undefined. The nonzero-jump hypothesis of [F1] fails, so this model does not contradict that theorem.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $g_0\\in\\mathbb R$, $v_0,c,\\kappa>0$, and distinct positive molar entropies $s_\\alpha,s_\\beta$. Boundary empty: The empty boundary is outside the explicitly stated hypotheses or no such case is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $g_0\\in\\mathbb R$, $v_0,c,\\kappa>0$, and distinct positive molar entropies $s_\\alpha,s_\\beta$. Use units $g_0\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $v_0\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, $c\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\kappa\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$. On a common open chart define\n$$\ng_i(T,p)=g_0-s_i(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_0(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2,\n$$\nwith $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$. Let $G_i=n_i g_i$ and adopt $S_i=-\\partial_TG_i$, $V_i=\\partial_pG_i$, $\\mu_i=\\partial_{n_i}G_i$, and $U_i=G_i+TS_i-pV_i$. This gives same-tag scaling and smooth local Gibbs charts. At $(T_0,p_0)$ the molar Gibbs values agree and the molar-volume jump is zero; the entropies differ.",
